to those segmented blood cells.

06:31:09.720 --> 06:31:12.360
To start, we get infected by malaria

06:31:12.360 --> 06:31:14.800
once we are beaten by a mosquito.

06:31:14.800 --> 06:31:19.240
These mosquito bites usually lead to the passing

06:31:19.240 --> 06:31:23.640
of Plasmodium pacifarum parasite into our blood system.

06:31:23.640 --> 06:31:27.060
And so to diagnose whether a particular person

06:31:27.060 --> 06:31:30.040
has got the malaria or not,

06:31:30.040 --> 06:31:33.000
it's important to get that person's blood.

06:31:33.000 --> 06:31:35.280
Here you see how the medical practitioner

06:31:35.280 --> 06:31:37.400
has to select the finger to puncture,

06:31:37.400 --> 06:31:39.480
usually the third or fourth finger.

06:31:39.480 --> 06:31:41.200
Then to obtain this blood,

06:31:41.200 --> 06:31:44.040
you puncture the side of the ball of the finger.

06:31:44.040 --> 06:31:46.160
In the case the blood doesn't well up,

06:31:46.160 --> 06:31:49.000
you would have to generally squeeze the finger

06:31:49.000 --> 06:31:50.840
so you can obtain the blood.

06:31:50.840 --> 06:31:53.760
Then always grab the slide by its edge.

06:31:53.760 --> 06:31:55.600
So to control the size of the blood,

06:31:55.600 --> 06:31:59.960
drop on the slide, touch the finger to the slide from below.

06:31:59.960 --> 06:32:02.320
From that cdc.gov website,

06:32:02.320 --> 06:32:05.560
we'll get to this microbe notes website

06:32:05.560 --> 06:32:08.600
where we'll get more colored images.

06:32:08.600 --> 06:32:12.160
Now we've obtained the patient's blood or the person's blood

06:32:12.160 --> 06:32:14.600
and there are two possibilities.

06:32:14.600 --> 06:32:17.520
One is getting a thin smear like this

06:32:17.520 --> 06:32:19.600
and one is getting a thick smear.

06:32:19.600 --> 06:32:22.040
In our case, our data set is gotten

06:32:22.040 --> 06:32:24.760
from a thin smear like this.

06:32:24.760 --> 06:32:26.660
So here we have this thin smear,

06:32:26.660 --> 06:32:29.700
which when passed on a microscope,

06:32:29.700 --> 06:32:31.340
produces images like this.

06:32:31.340 --> 06:32:34.180
So here we have thin blood film

06:32:34.180 --> 06:32:36.660
and we have thick blood film.

06:32:36.660 --> 06:32:40.380
From this point, we now segment the cells.

06:32:40.380 --> 06:32:41.980
The cells are now segmented

06:32:41.980 --> 06:32:45.980
and that's how we obtain an image like this

06:32:45.980 --> 06:32:49.980
or a segmented cell image like this,

06:32:49.980 --> 06:32:52.660
which now could be used by a model

06:32:52.660 --> 06:32:55.320
to predict whether that patient

06:32:55.320 --> 06:32:58.220
has got the malaria parasite or not.

06:32:58.220 --> 06:33:00.180
It should also be noted that we're dealing

06:33:00.180 --> 06:33:02.060
with a classification problem

06:33:02.060 --> 06:33:06.940
since our output can only take two discrete values.

06:33:06.940 --> 06:33:09.020
This type of classification problem

06:33:09.020 --> 06:33:12.220
is known as binary classification.

06:33:12.220 --> 06:33:13.780
Since throughout the section,

06:33:13.780 --> 06:33:16.060
we'll be dealing with image data,

06:33:16.060 --> 06:33:20.260
it's important we understand how image data is represented.

06:33:20.260 --> 06:33:23.820
So here we have this image of this bird

06:33:23.820 --> 06:33:28.820
and then when you zoom in, this is what you get.

06:33:30.220 --> 06:33:32.620
If you zoom in again, you should have this.

06:33:32.620 --> 06:33:36.100
So this tells us that this image right here

06:33:36.100 --> 06:33:41.100
is formed by combining all these little boxes

06:33:41.900 --> 06:33:43.380
we have right here.

06:33:43.380 --> 06:33:46.180
And these boxes are known as pixels.

06:33:47.180 --> 06:33:50.680
That said, here we could localize this pixel.

06:33:50.680 --> 06:33:53.300
We have this pixel, this one, this one,

06:33:53.300 --> 06:33:54.580
and so on and so forth.

06:33:54.580 --> 06:33:59.500
So basically, this image is made of all these little boxes

06:33:59.500 --> 06:34:00.860
which we call pixels.

06:34:02.020 --> 06:34:04.960
If now we take this image from our dataset,

06:34:04.960 --> 06:34:07.700
you could see that, notice from here,

06:34:07.700 --> 06:34:12.700
we have 86 by 82 PX, 86 by 82 pixel image,

06:34:14.260 --> 06:34:16.860
meaning that we have 86.

06:34:16.860 --> 06:34:19.020
Notice how here we go up to 86.

06:34:19.020 --> 06:34:22.820
So at this point, we have 86 and the width.

06:34:22.820 --> 06:34:25.380
So we have 86 of these boxes.

06:34:25.380 --> 06:34:27.380
If you have to go from this point

06:34:27.380 --> 06:34:30.260
to this other point right here,

06:34:30.260 --> 06:34:33.260
we would go through 86 of these pixels.

06:34:33.260 --> 06:34:35.220
And then if you have to go from this point

06:34:35.220 --> 06:34:37.800
to this one right here, you have 82.

06:34:38.660 --> 06:34:42.340
Now check out what is displayed at this position.

06:34:42.340 --> 06:34:45.580
So as we move, you should be able to see what's displayed.

06:34:45.580 --> 06:34:48.060
You see that at this point, for example,

06:34:48.060 --> 06:34:53.060
we had position 85, 34.

06:34:53.380 --> 06:34:56.940
So we've gone 85 steps horizontally

06:34:56.940 --> 06:34:59.320
and then 34 steps vertically.

06:34:59.320 --> 06:35:02.980
We're considering our origin to be at this top left corner

06:35:02.980 --> 06:35:03.820
right here.

06:35:03.820 --> 06:35:08.820
So we have 85 steps and then 34 steps in this direction.

06:35:10.640 --> 06:35:12.900
And then you should be able to localize

06:35:12.900 --> 06:35:14.760
these pixels right here.

06:35:14.760 --> 06:35:18.300
So you could notice how all those pixels now,

06:35:18.300 --> 06:35:21.980
when combined, will be able to form this image.

06:35:21.980 --> 06:35:24.660
To reduce this, you see that it becomes less evident

06:35:24.660 --> 06:35:25.900
to notice those pixels.

06:35:25.900 --> 06:35:26.740
So that's it.

06:35:29.180 --> 06:35:32.340
It should also be noted that each of those pixels

06:35:32.340 --> 06:35:37.340
contain values ranging from zero to 255.

06:35:39.420 --> 06:35:43.120
So here we have values ranging between zero and 255.

06:35:43.120 --> 06:35:46.340
And for each and every pixel,

06:35:46.340 --> 06:35:48.380
we have three different components,

06:35:48.380 --> 06:35:52.200
the red, the green, and the blue.

06:35:52.200 --> 06:35:54.700
So if we break this image here

06:35:54.700 --> 06:35:56.140
into these three different components,

06:35:56.140 --> 06:35:57.680
we would have something like this.

06:35:57.680 --> 06:36:01.000
Now note that these values are actually normalized.

06:36:01.000 --> 06:36:03.340
So basically what they've done here

06:36:03.340 --> 06:36:05.140
is they've taken all these values

06:36:05.140 --> 06:36:08.920
and then they've divided by 255.

06:36:08.920 --> 06:36:10.700
So if you want to get the un-normalized values,

06:36:10.700 --> 06:36:12.420
you should be able to take this

06:36:12.420 --> 06:36:17.220
and multiply by 255 to obtain the original values.

06:36:17.220 --> 06:36:19.900
That said, notice how at this point here,

06:36:19.900 --> 06:36:22.140
this position, we have this.

06:36:22.140 --> 06:36:26.460
At this position, we have this and this.

06:36:26.460 --> 06:36:29.100
And so we could represent image data

06:36:29.100 --> 06:36:32.780
in terms of the height, the width,

06:36:34.100 --> 06:36:36.260
and the number of channels,

06:36:36.260 --> 06:36:39.020
which is in this case equal to three.

06:36:39.020 --> 06:36:41.060
So we have height,

06:36:41.060 --> 06:36:43.700
that is the shape of our image tensor,

06:36:43.700 --> 06:36:46.300
height by width by three.

06:36:47.660 --> 06:36:51.040
And then another common format is the grayscale format.

06:36:51.040 --> 06:36:52.580
With the grayscale format,

06:36:52.580 --> 06:36:55.020
we, this number of channels equal one.

06:36:55.020 --> 06:36:57.300
We have just one channel,

06:36:57.300 --> 06:37:01.900
and it could be represented as a 2D tensor.

06:37:01.900 --> 06:37:04.340
Another interesting point to notice,

06:37:04.340 --> 06:37:08.080
though we've said for each position or for each pixel,

06:37:08.080 --> 06:37:12.160
we have a given value per each per component.

06:37:12.160 --> 06:37:16.380
We have to note that all these values

06:37:16.380 --> 06:37:21.260
fall between black and white.

06:37:21.260 --> 06:37:25.180
That is for black, if you want to get a black value,

06:37:25.180 --> 06:37:27.900
that the pixel value will be zero.

06:37:27.900 --> 06:37:32.900
And then for white, the pixel value will be 255.

06:37:33.140 --> 06:37:35.920
Obviously, when we normalize these values,

06:37:35.920 --> 06:37:39.500
here we're going from zero up to one.

06:37:39.500 --> 06:37:40.340
So that's it.

