and we take this off.

06:59:35.140 --> 06:59:36.760
So that should be fine now.

06:59:36.760 --> 06:59:41.160
We have this images and the corresponding levels.

06:59:41.160 --> 06:59:44.680
You can see how this is pasteurized.

06:59:44.680 --> 06:59:46.480
This, for example, is uninfected,

06:59:46.480 --> 06:59:48.840
but this isn't very clear.

06:59:48.840 --> 06:59:53.720
So let's take off this, put that axis.

06:59:53.720 --> 06:59:56.220
We set this to off, run that.

06:59:57.320 --> 07:00:01.120
And that looks better, but we could make this better.

07:00:01.120 --> 07:00:05.080
If you wanna know which of these is the level zero

07:00:05.080 --> 07:00:08.720
and which is the level one, let's take this from here.

07:00:08.720 --> 07:00:11.040
And then we print out to zero.

07:00:11.040 --> 07:00:12.880
You see that this level zero,

07:00:12.880 --> 07:00:17.880
it's given to us as pasteurized and one is uninfected.

07:00:18.120 --> 07:00:18.960
So that's it.

07:00:22.560 --> 07:00:25.640
At this point, we're gonna dive into data processing.

07:00:25.640 --> 07:00:27.720
Our data processing unit from now

07:00:27.720 --> 07:00:29.260
will be made of two parts.

07:00:29.260 --> 07:00:31.800
The first part will be the resizing part.

07:00:31.800 --> 07:00:36.800
So if we have an input image of say 102 by 102

07:00:40.440 --> 07:00:43.560
as width and height, we could have this.

07:00:43.560 --> 07:00:47.040
We're gonna transform this into an image

07:00:47.040 --> 07:00:50.200
of a fixed width and fixed height.

07:00:50.200 --> 07:00:53.440
Now that said, all our images,

07:00:53.440 --> 07:00:56.320
irrespective of their width and heights,

07:00:56.320 --> 07:01:00.500
will now have just one width and one height.

07:01:00.500 --> 07:01:04.880
In this case, we'll consider an image size of 224.

07:01:04.880 --> 07:01:08.560
So our width and height will be converted to 224.

07:01:08.560 --> 07:01:10.560
And subsequently, we'll see why

07:01:10.560 --> 07:01:15.100
we are picking this image size right here.

07:01:16.300 --> 07:01:21.300
After this resizing, our next part will be on normalization.

07:01:22.280 --> 07:01:25.720
So we'll have an input image which will normalize

07:01:25.720 --> 07:01:30.720
such that all the data falls in a given range.

07:01:31.200 --> 07:01:34.920
Here we have the standardization process

07:01:34.920 --> 07:01:38.640
and we have the normalization process.

07:01:38.640 --> 07:01:42.120
In this case, we are gonna use a normalization process

07:01:42.120 --> 07:01:44.040
and we're gonna explain why.

07:01:44.040 --> 07:01:47.800
In the previous example on the car price prediction,

07:01:47.800 --> 07:01:50.880
we actually work with standardization.

07:01:50.880 --> 07:01:54.760
In standardization, each value is subtracted

07:01:54.760 --> 07:01:58.720
from the mean and then divided by the standard deviation.

07:01:58.720 --> 07:02:03.720
That's actually each value on each and every column

07:02:03.940 --> 07:02:08.280
of X right here is being standardized

07:02:08.280 --> 07:02:11.880
based on its mean and the standard deviation.

07:02:11.880 --> 07:02:16.120
As you'll notice, these values, for example,

07:02:16.120 --> 07:02:18.000
are normally distributed.

07:02:18.000 --> 07:02:21.640
That is, we have a mean value on average value

07:02:21.640 --> 07:02:25.720
and we have a standard deviation or range of values

07:02:25.720 --> 07:02:29.220
where most of times our values will fall in.

07:02:29.220 --> 07:02:31.600
So it's gonna look bell shaped like this

07:02:31.600 --> 07:02:33.080
as we've seen before.

07:02:33.080 --> 07:02:36.320
We have the mean and then we have a certain

07:02:36.320 --> 07:02:39.520
standard deviation right here.

07:02:39.520 --> 07:02:41.400
And that's why if you look at X2,

07:02:41.400 --> 07:02:45.160
you wouldn't have say 5,600, 7,100

07:02:45.160 --> 07:02:48.000
and then here, for example, come and have a value

07:02:48.000 --> 07:02:53.000
like say 12, this isn't very typical since

07:02:53.000 --> 07:02:56.000
most of the times these values fall under a given range

07:02:56.000 --> 07:02:59.880
and there's a certain average value

07:02:59.880 --> 07:03:04.000
for which most other values just fall around.

07:03:04.000 --> 07:03:08.560
So that's why it's typical to have these kinds of values.

07:03:08.560 --> 07:03:09.680
Let's take this off.

07:03:10.560 --> 07:03:13.520
For the next, you'll notice how these values fall

07:03:13.520 --> 07:03:17.800
under a given kind of range and with this too.

07:03:17.800 --> 07:03:20.640
Now, this is for when we deal with standardization

07:03:20.640 --> 07:03:24.240
and that's why previously we used this actually.

07:03:24.240 --> 07:03:26.920
Now, in the case of image data,

07:03:26.920 --> 07:03:30.600
the choice of whether to standardize or to normalize

07:03:30.600 --> 07:03:33.560
will depend on the kind of data we're dealing with.

07:03:33.560 --> 07:03:38.560
So if we have images where most of its pixels

07:03:38.560 --> 07:03:42.360
revolve around a particular mean value,

07:03:42.360 --> 07:03:44.960
then we'll wanna standardize

07:03:44.960 --> 07:03:48.960
and if this image is made of pixels

07:03:48.960 --> 07:03:52.960
where their values are mostly different from one another,

07:03:52.960 --> 07:03:56.240
then we would want to normalize.

07:03:56.240 --> 07:04:00.280
Now, in our case, as we continue in this section,

07:04:00.280 --> 07:04:02.400
we are gonna go with normalization,

07:04:02.400 --> 07:04:06.840
that is we will have X minus X mean, which is zero,

07:04:06.840 --> 07:04:11.400
divided by X max, which is 255,

07:04:11.400 --> 07:04:13.960
minus X mean, which is zero.

07:04:13.960 --> 07:04:17.320
So simply do X divided by 255.

07:04:17.320 --> 07:04:19.920
So we'll normalize the inputs

07:04:19.920 --> 07:04:22.480
before passing them into our model.

07:04:22.480 --> 07:04:25.320
Now, it should be noted that for some other datasets like,

07:04:25.320 --> 07:04:29.720
for example, ImageNet or ImageNet,

07:04:29.720 --> 07:04:31.280
we have ImageNet dataset,

07:04:31.280 --> 07:04:36.280
or the RDE20K image segmentation dataset,

07:04:36.280 --> 07:04:41.280
they have their known mean and standard deviation values.

07:04:43.000 --> 07:04:47.000
Nonetheless, for whatever problem you have to deal with,

07:04:47.000 --> 07:04:52.000
you may work with standardization or normalization,

07:04:52.160 --> 07:04:57.160
experiment for yourself and see which one works better.

07:04:57.760 --> 07:05:01.520
Now that said, since we're dealing with a TensorFlow data API,

07:05:01.520 --> 07:05:03.880
we are gonna use this map method

07:05:03.880 --> 07:05:07.120
to help us in this pre-processing.

07:05:07.120 --> 07:05:09.280
So there we go, we have trained dataset.

07:05:09.280 --> 07:05:11.520
We'll start first with the resizing.

07:05:11.520 --> 07:05:15.360
Trained dataset equals trained dataset.map,

07:05:15.360 --> 07:05:17.920
and then we'll call this resizing method,

07:05:17.920 --> 07:05:19.480
which we shall define.

07:05:19.480 --> 07:05:22.120
Let's add this and put this up here.

07:05:22.120 --> 07:05:24.760
So we have our resizing method.

07:05:24.760 --> 07:05:29.280
This method takes in the image and the level.

07:05:29.280 --> 07:05:32.920
So we have our image and the level.

07:05:32.920 --> 07:05:35.160
And the level right here.

07:05:35.160 --> 07:05:38.440
But note that we're doing processing on only the image.

07:05:38.440 --> 07:05:39.960
So we're gonna pass this in,

07:05:39.960 --> 07:05:44.600
and then we're gonna resize this using the resize method,

07:05:44.600 --> 07:05:47.720
which comes with TensorFlow images right here.

07:05:47.720 --> 07:05:49.280
So we have TensorFlow image,

07:05:49.280 --> 07:05:52.080
and then we have this resize method.

07:05:52.080 --> 07:05:54.360
Here you could see the arguments which are passed.

07:05:54.360 --> 07:05:58.080
For now we'll focus on the image and the size.

07:05:58.080 --> 07:06:00.560
We will also note that you could change the method

07:06:00.560 --> 07:06:02.120
using resizing.

07:06:02.120 --> 07:06:06.920
But for now we use this default values given to us.

07:06:07.840 --> 07:06:08.680
So that's it.

07:06:08.680 --> 07:06:12.440
We are gonna return the image.

07:06:12.440 --> 07:06:17.440
So let's have our TensorFlow image dot resize,

07:06:18.440 --> 07:06:20.160
and it takes in our image.

07:06:20.160 --> 07:06:24.320
And then we have the image size.

07:06:24.320 --> 07:06:27.880
So we'll have in size by in size.

07:06:27.880 --> 07:06:30.120
Note that we are gonna define the in size here.

07:06:30.120 --> 07:06:35.120
So we'll have in size equal to 124.

07:06:35.480 --> 07:06:37.200
So we have this resizing.

07:06:37.200 --> 07:06:39.080
Our image is returned to this,

07:06:39.080 --> 07:06:43.920
224 by 24, 24 of a shaped image.

07:06:43.920 --> 07:06:45.480
Now we have resized this.

07:06:45.480 --> 07:06:47.520
We just put out a level.

07:06:47.520 --> 07:06:51.040
So here again, we just basically taking the image,

07:06:51.040 --> 07:06:55.040
resizing it, and then the level remains the same.

07:06:55.040 --> 07:06:56.200
So we could run this,

07:06:56.200 --> 07:06:58.200
and then we have our trained data set,

07:06:58.200 --> 07:06:59.840
which now has been resized.

07:07:00.720 --> 07:07:01.560
That's it.

07:07:01.560 --> 07:07:06.560
We could say for data in train data set,

07:07:07.160 --> 07:07:09.720
we're gonna print out, oh, let's take one value.

07:07:09.720 --> 07:07:12.280
So yeah, we take just one value,

07:07:12.280 --> 07:07:15.920
and we have our, let's say image,

07:07:15.920 --> 07:07:19.440
actually image and the level.

07:07:20.400 --> 07:07:25.000
We print out, we print out the image and the level.

07:07:25.000 --> 07:07:27.600
Let's run that, and there we go.

07:07:27.600 --> 07:07:29.920
You'll notice that the shape of this image

07:07:29.920 --> 07:07:32.760
is gonna be 224 by 224,

07:07:32.760 --> 07:07:36.480
irrespective of whichever, or which image we pick,

07:07:36.480 --> 07:07:40.040
and then we have its level, which remains unchanged.

07:07:40.040 --> 07:07:43.200
Notice how this is a plot 32.

07:07:43.200 --> 07:07:48.120
Unlike previously where we had an unsigned int.

07:07:48.120 --> 07:07:50.720
Now you could always do casting right here

07:07:50.720 --> 07:07:55.720
to modify this depending on what you're working on.

07:07:55.720 --> 07:07:58.040
So that said, we're done with the resizing.

07:07:58.040 --> 07:08:01.520
We could now look at reskilling.

07:08:01.520 --> 07:08:06.520
So let's put this as resizing and reskilling.

07:08:06.600 --> 07:08:11.400
So we have resizing, resize, let's just say resize, reskill.

07:08:11.400 --> 07:08:13.560
So our resizing reskill function is such that

07:08:13.560 --> 07:08:17.560
after resizing, we reskill by dividing by 255.

07:08:19.000 --> 07:08:22.360
So we divide all our values by 255,

07:08:22.360 --> 07:08:23.920
our level remains the same.

07:08:23.920 --> 07:08:28.920
We run this, we run that, here is resize, reskill.

07:08:29.400 --> 07:08:33.000
There we go, we run that, and then we have this.

07:08:33.000 --> 07:08:34.360
So that's fine.

07:08:34.360 --> 07:08:37.240
Just as we did before, that was in the previous section,

07:08:37.240 --> 07:08:39.120
we're gonna shuffle our data,

07:08:39.120 --> 07:08:40.920
we're gonna put this in batches,

07:08:40.920 --> 07:08:43.640
and then we'll do prefetching.

07:08:43.640 --> 07:08:54.640
So all this was explained in the previous section.

07:08:54.760 --> 07:08:56.880
We are now ready to build our model,

07:08:56.880 --> 07:08:59.160
and up to now neural networks

07:08:59.160 --> 07:09:01.480
have been performing quite well.

07:09:01.480 --> 07:09:05.000
We had seen previously that if we have three neurons,

07:09:05.000 --> 07:09:08.960
that's one, two, three in the input,

07:09:08.960 --> 07:09:13.480
and in the output we have three neurons too,

07:09:13.480 --> 07:09:17.480
then there'll be nine different connections here,

07:09:17.480 --> 07:09:20.960
and hence nine different weights plus the bias.

07:09:21.960 --> 07:09:24.200
But for this example, let's consider only the weights.

07:09:24.200 --> 07:09:26.720
So we have nine different parameters

07:09:26.720 --> 07:09:29.300
for three inputs, three outputs.

07:09:30.360 --> 07:09:33.840
And if here we have five, considering only the weights,

07:09:33.840 --> 07:09:35.700
we would have three times five,

07:09:35.700 --> 07:09:39.080
that's 15 different parameters.

07:09:39.080 --> 07:09:42.320
Now, if we're dealing with an image like this one,

07:09:42.320 --> 07:09:44.040
so let's consider this image,

07:09:44.040 --> 07:09:49.040
which is 224 by 224 by three image,

07:09:52.960 --> 07:09:57.960
three for the three different red, green, and blue channels.

07:09:58.040 --> 07:10:00.800
So we have this input image now.

07:10:00.800 --> 07:10:03.880
So unlike previous examples,

07:10:03.880 --> 07:10:06.920
where we would have features,

07:10:06.920 --> 07:10:09.660
or specifically in this case input features,

07:10:09.660 --> 07:10:13.620
we do not contain as many elements as this.

07:10:13.620 --> 07:10:16.120
We now have this case where if we want to count

07:10:16.120 --> 07:10:19.600
the number of features or the number of pixels,

07:10:19.600 --> 07:10:24.600
we would have 150,528 different input features

07:10:26.560 --> 07:10:28.200
to take into consideration.

07:10:28.200 --> 07:10:33.200
Hence, instead of having a total number of three right here,

07:10:33.200 --> 07:10:38.200
we are going to have 150,528 different values.

07:10:40.360 --> 07:10:42.820
And if we want to do the same competition

07:10:42.820 --> 07:10:44.920
which got us this number of parameters,

07:10:44.920 --> 07:10:48.200
we would have 150,000 times three,

07:10:48.200 --> 07:10:52.880
that's approximately 450,000 different parameters.

07:10:54.040 --> 07:10:58.240
Now, what if we modify this number of neurons right here

07:10:58.240 --> 07:11:03.240
and take say 1,000 neurons in this output right here?

07:11:03.480 --> 07:11:06.560
We would see that we'll move from 150,000

07:11:06.560 --> 07:11:11.560
or rather from 450,000 to 150 million different parameters

07:11:14.000 --> 07:11:16.260
where each and every parameter

07:11:16.260 --> 07:11:19.960
has to be trained and optimized.

07:11:19.960 --> 07:11:23.680
This becomes clear that deep neural networks like this

07:11:23.680 --> 07:11:28.680
are better still than layers of fully connected layers

07:11:29.840 --> 07:11:32.080
aren't scalable.

07:11:32.080 --> 07:11:35.080
Since when we increase the number of features,

07:11:35.080 --> 07:11:38.960
the total number of parameters also increase considerably.

07:11:38.960 --> 07:11:41.720
Hence, we need to build a type of layer

07:11:41.720 --> 07:11:46.720
unlike this one where each neuron isn't connected

07:11:47.060 --> 07:11:49.880
to all the previous neurons.

07:11:49.880 --> 07:11:54.880
And this layer happens to be the convolutional layer.

07:11:55.200 --> 07:11:57.560
In order to better visualize this,

07:11:57.560 --> 07:12:01.840
we'll use this demo platform from Wrightson University.

07:12:01.840 --> 07:12:05.400
So here we'll put in a figure, let's say four,

07:12:05.400 --> 07:12:09.880
and then we'll see exactly how this confnet work.

07:12:09.880 --> 07:12:14.120
So we have this and then we have this input right here.

07:12:14.120 --> 07:12:18.320
So we get this input, we have some weights

07:12:18.320 --> 07:12:20.880
and then this output features.

07:12:20.880 --> 07:12:25.880
Note how to obtain this particular pixel right here,

07:12:26.600 --> 07:12:31.600
only a few of these inputs are used.

07:12:32.120 --> 07:12:36.200
And so yeah, we call this the receptive field.

07:12:36.200 --> 07:12:39.580
As you could see, we have the receptive field right here.

07:12:40.720 --> 07:12:42.560
And if we take this other example,

07:12:42.560 --> 07:12:44.160
you'll see it's on receptive field.

07:12:44.160 --> 07:12:45.720
You see if to get this value,

07:12:45.720 --> 07:12:50.720
only these four values you see below, actually this one,

07:12:50.720 --> 07:12:54.840
this, this and this, as you could see here, let's scroll back.

07:12:54.840 --> 07:12:59.480
Okay, so only those four values have a role to play

07:12:59.480 --> 07:13:03.200
when it comes to giving us the corresponding value

07:13:03.200 --> 07:13:04.920
we have here.

07:13:04.920 --> 07:13:06.760
And so unlike with a dense layer,

07:13:06.760 --> 07:13:08.160
where to obtain this value,

07:13:08.160 --> 07:13:12.680
we needed to link this to each and every previous neuron.

07:13:12.680 --> 07:13:17.680
Now only just some neurons in this neurons receptive field

07:13:19.960 --> 07:13:22.080
play a role in getting this value.

07:13:22.080 --> 07:13:24.880
Another great tool for better understanding

07:13:24.880 --> 07:13:29.200
the convolutional layer is this CNN explainer.

