Actually CNN stands for convolutional neural networks.

07:13:32.360 --> 07:13:36.320
This is created by J. Wang, Robert, Omar,

07:13:36.320 --> 07:13:40.280
Park, Das, Frank, Kang and Polo.

07:13:40.280 --> 07:13:42.660
We are really grateful for this tool.

07:13:42.660 --> 07:13:44.500
Before getting back to the explainer,

07:13:44.500 --> 07:13:46.360
let's take this example, right?

07:13:46.360 --> 07:13:48.680
Here we have a four by four image.

07:13:48.680 --> 07:13:51.240
So we have 16 different pixels.

07:13:51.240 --> 07:13:52.880
If we flatten out those pixels,

07:13:52.880 --> 07:13:55.800
that's if we put those pixels out like this,

07:13:55.800 --> 07:13:57.880
such that we have those inputs.

07:13:57.880 --> 07:14:01.800
And then in the output, we have four different neurons,

07:14:01.800 --> 07:14:05.680
then here would have 16 by four connections

07:14:05.680 --> 07:14:08.760
that will have 64 different parameters,

07:14:08.760 --> 07:14:13.760
excluding the bias, but with the conv layer,

07:14:13.960 --> 07:14:18.960
we could leave from this four by four to just a two by two.

07:14:20.400 --> 07:14:23.960
So we could leave from this four by four to this two by two

07:14:25.040 --> 07:14:28.920
with just nine parameters.

07:14:28.920 --> 07:14:33.920
So we would have what we call a kernel right here or filter.

07:14:34.560 --> 07:14:37.820
So we have this filter, which is three by three,

07:14:37.820 --> 07:14:39.760
which actually corresponds to our weights,

07:14:39.760 --> 07:14:41.200
which we've seen already.

07:14:41.200 --> 07:14:45.720
This kernel here, kernel size three,

07:14:45.720 --> 07:14:49.800
will produce this output of two by two,

07:14:49.800 --> 07:14:54.160
which when we flatten out can give us this output right here.

07:14:54.160 --> 07:14:56.800
And so instead of working with 64 parameters,

07:14:56.800 --> 07:14:59.000
we're working with nine parameters.

07:14:59.000 --> 07:15:01.680
If we want to replicate that same example

07:15:01.680 --> 07:15:04.540
in the CNN explainer, here's what we get.

07:15:04.540 --> 07:15:06.720
We have this input right here,

07:15:06.720 --> 07:15:08.960
which produces this output.

07:15:08.960 --> 07:15:13.040
Now, notice how we specify that a kernel size equal to three.

07:15:13.040 --> 07:15:15.360
And because a kernel size equal to three,

07:15:15.360 --> 07:15:17.320
we are able to get this output.

07:15:17.320 --> 07:15:19.880
But how is this output gotten?

07:15:19.880 --> 07:15:23.180
You would see that at this top left corner,

07:15:23.180 --> 07:15:25.400
we are going to feed our kernel,

07:15:25.400 --> 07:15:27.280
which is of size three by three.

07:15:27.280 --> 07:15:29.700
So we put in our kernel right here,

07:15:29.700 --> 07:15:34.120
and then we take each and every value of our kernel

07:15:34.120 --> 07:15:38.240
and multiply it with a corresponding value in the input.

07:15:38.240 --> 07:15:40.160
To obtain the first value,

07:15:40.160 --> 07:15:43.760
you see how we pass this kernel on the input.

07:15:43.760 --> 07:15:46.620
So at this top left position, we pass this kernel.

07:15:46.620 --> 07:15:50.000
Notice how we have a three by three kernel,

07:15:50.000 --> 07:15:51.340
which is passed on this input.

07:15:51.340 --> 07:15:53.760
And we have the output right here.

07:15:53.760 --> 07:15:57.920
Then to get the next position, to get this next output,

07:15:57.920 --> 07:16:00.600
you see how this kernel is passed

07:16:00.600 --> 07:16:04.500
on this next part of the input.

07:16:04.500 --> 07:16:06.040
And the way we get this next part

07:16:06.040 --> 07:16:09.460
is by simply sliding through the image.

07:16:09.460 --> 07:16:10.920
You see how we're left from this,

07:16:10.920 --> 07:16:13.080
we slide that through the image and we got this,

07:16:13.080 --> 07:16:14.640
we got this next output.

07:16:14.640 --> 07:16:16.780
And since we've gotten to the end,

07:16:16.780 --> 07:16:19.840
we move to the next position, which is this,

07:16:19.840 --> 07:16:24.080
we get this next value right here.

07:16:24.080 --> 07:16:26.460
And then from here, we slide again to this end,

07:16:26.460 --> 07:16:28.360
and we finally get this value.

07:16:28.360 --> 07:16:31.640
So that's how we get all this outputs

07:16:31.640 --> 07:16:35.480
from the inputs and the kernel or the filter.

07:16:36.360 --> 07:16:38.680
We can now go ahead and increase this input size.

07:16:38.680 --> 07:16:40.680
Let's take, for example, seven.

07:16:40.680 --> 07:16:43.040
You see, we have a input size of seven,

07:16:43.040 --> 07:16:45.400
seven by seven input image.

07:16:45.400 --> 07:16:48.720
And then we have this output five by five.

07:16:48.720 --> 07:16:50.520
The reason why we have this five by five

07:16:50.520 --> 07:16:52.840
is because we have this kernel size of three.

07:16:52.840 --> 07:16:54.800
If we get to increase the kernel size of five,

07:16:54.800 --> 07:16:56.680
you see our output reduces.

07:16:56.680 --> 07:16:58.680
You see, when the kernel size gets to six,

07:16:58.680 --> 07:17:00.280
our output reduces.

07:17:00.280 --> 07:17:02.880
Let's take this back to three.

07:17:02.880 --> 07:17:04.280
So you take back to three,

07:17:04.280 --> 07:17:06.680
and this input of seven by seven

07:17:06.680 --> 07:17:08.560
gives an output of five by five.

07:17:08.560 --> 07:17:11.320
We see how when we get to this, we have that output,

07:17:11.320 --> 07:17:15.040
move, slide, slide, slide, slide,

07:17:15.040 --> 07:17:18.640
move to this next here, slide, slide, slide,

07:17:18.640 --> 07:17:22.020
and so on and so forth, right up to the end.

07:17:22.020 --> 07:17:26.280
So from here, we see how the size of the receptive field

07:17:26.280 --> 07:17:29.960
of each of these outputs equal to three,

07:17:29.960 --> 07:17:32.640
which is actually our kernel size.

07:17:32.640 --> 07:17:37.400
The next thing to notice is reducing the kernel size

07:17:37.400 --> 07:17:42.400
permits us extract more features from the inputs.

07:17:42.640 --> 07:17:46.080
You would see that since we have this input seven by seven,

07:17:46.080 --> 07:17:49.080
with kernel size of three, we have the output five by five.

07:17:49.080 --> 07:17:52.440
So we've extracted much more features from this input

07:17:52.440 --> 07:17:55.880
as compared to when we push a kernel size of six.

07:17:55.880 --> 07:18:00.400
Here we extract less features from this inputs.

07:18:00.400 --> 07:18:04.000
Now, though using a smaller kernel size

07:18:04.000 --> 07:18:09.000
permits us extract much more complex information

07:18:09.440 --> 07:18:12.820
or complex features from the inputs,

07:18:12.820 --> 07:18:14.920
working with larger kernels

07:18:14.920 --> 07:18:19.080
permit us extract larger input features.

07:18:19.080 --> 07:18:22.600
One logical question which may come to your mind is,

07:18:22.600 --> 07:18:27.600
how do I get the size of this output feature map right here?

07:18:29.080 --> 07:18:32.320
To get this, we'll use this formula

07:18:32.320 --> 07:18:37.320
where this output width is equals the input width

07:18:37.440 --> 07:18:40.640
minus the filter size plus one.

07:18:40.640 --> 07:18:43.720
If we take this example, we have an input width of seven

07:18:44.760 --> 07:18:49.760
minus a filter size of three plus one, this gives us five.

07:18:50.000 --> 07:18:52.320
So that's how we obtain this right here.

07:18:52.320 --> 07:18:56.600
Now, if we take, say, kernel size,

07:18:56.600 --> 07:19:00.720
let's take the kernel size to be four, for example.

07:19:00.720 --> 07:19:05.720
In that case, we have seven minus the kernel size four,

07:19:08.140 --> 07:19:11.820
which is three plus one, which gives us an output of four.

07:19:11.820 --> 07:19:14.800
And that's how we obtain this output right here.

07:19:14.800 --> 07:19:18.340
Now, in a case where you're designing a convolutional layer

07:19:18.340 --> 07:19:22.600
and you want to get a particular feature map size,

07:19:22.600 --> 07:19:24.280
say, for example, in this case,

07:19:24.280 --> 07:19:28.440
if you want to get an output size of three,

07:19:29.440 --> 07:19:33.020
then all you need to do is specify this kernel size

07:19:33.020 --> 07:19:36.960
to be equal to five and you should get this output.

07:19:36.960 --> 07:19:38.400
Now, in some cases,

07:19:38.400 --> 07:19:42.680
you may want to have a particular feature map shape

07:19:42.680 --> 07:19:46.020
that using just the kernel size,

07:19:46.020 --> 07:19:48.400
you wouldn't be able to get that.

07:19:48.400 --> 07:19:52.840
And so to match up with a particular output shape

07:19:52.840 --> 07:19:55.200
would include a pattern.

07:19:55.200 --> 07:19:56.640
Let's look at this pattern.

07:19:56.640 --> 07:19:58.760
You see, we have a seven by seven.

07:19:58.760 --> 07:20:02.840
And when we take this to one, you see that we go from

07:20:02.840 --> 07:20:05.740
seven by seven to now nine by nine.

07:20:05.740 --> 07:20:06.940
Notice how it's written here.

07:20:06.940 --> 07:20:09.100
After pattern, we have nine by nine.

07:20:09.100 --> 07:20:11.380
So we'll leave from this seven by seven.

07:20:11.380 --> 07:20:14.760
Let's take this in this box right here, this internal box.

07:20:14.760 --> 07:20:17.160
And then the pattern is also nine by nine.

07:20:17.160 --> 07:20:19.560
Notice how after doing the pattern,

07:20:19.560 --> 07:20:22.280
and if we pair around with our kernel size,

07:20:22.280 --> 07:20:25.960
we go from one, one to eight, eight.

07:20:25.960 --> 07:20:28.000
So we go from one, one to eight, eight.

07:20:28.000 --> 07:20:33.000
But with zero pattern and the input size of seven,

07:20:34.020 --> 07:20:37.360
you see, we go two, three, four, and six.

07:20:37.360 --> 07:20:39.080
So we cannot go up to eight, eight.

07:20:39.080 --> 07:20:41.480
We're gonna have an output of eight, eight

07:20:41.480 --> 07:20:43.240
when the pattern is zero.

07:20:43.240 --> 07:20:47.680
Whereas when we increase the pattern by one here,

07:20:47.680 --> 07:20:50.600
increase the pattern by one, that's one.

07:20:50.600 --> 07:20:52.640
You see that we could go from,

07:20:52.640 --> 07:20:57.640
we could get an output of say, let's put a count to three.

07:20:58.040 --> 07:21:03.040
We could get an output of seven, of eight,

07:21:04.160 --> 07:21:06.900
you see, up to one.

07:21:06.900 --> 07:21:07.960
So that's it.

07:21:07.960 --> 07:21:11.400
Now, or we understand how this pattern works.

07:21:11.400 --> 07:21:14.600
Basically, we just have our input

07:21:14.600 --> 07:21:19.200
and then we add this surrounding elements.

07:21:19.200 --> 07:21:23.780
Right here, we just add our input image.

07:21:23.780 --> 07:21:27.260
Now, the pattern generally we use is the zero pattern,

07:21:27.260 --> 07:21:30.120
though there are other pattern methods,

07:21:30.120 --> 07:21:33.520
but the zero pattern is one of the most common

07:21:33.520 --> 07:21:34.900
since it's easy to use

07:21:34.900 --> 07:21:38.280
and it's computationally less expensive to work with.

07:21:38.280 --> 07:21:40.540
So that said, here's our pattern.

07:21:40.540 --> 07:21:42.560
And then we also have another advantage

07:21:42.560 --> 07:21:44.040
of working with a pattern,

07:21:44.040 --> 07:21:48.040
which is that of ensuring that the corner pixels

07:21:49.720 --> 07:21:52.700
have an influence on our output features

07:21:52.700 --> 07:21:53.920
which are generated.

07:21:53.920 --> 07:21:56.000
Now let's take this back to zero pattern.

07:21:56.000 --> 07:21:57.480
We have zero pattern

07:21:57.480 --> 07:22:00.800
and we have this input image right here.

07:22:00.800 --> 07:22:04.380
If we're doing an image in which most of the information

07:22:04.380 --> 07:22:07.380
or most of the relevant information is centered,

07:22:07.380 --> 07:22:09.280
then there is actually no issue

07:22:09.280 --> 07:22:14.020
since this photo right here will go through

07:22:14.020 --> 07:22:17.080
each and every pixel we have

07:22:17.080 --> 07:22:19.800
where we have this person and the image.

07:22:19.800 --> 07:22:24.040
Now, if we modify this image such that we have a person's,

07:22:24.040 --> 07:22:26.240
let's suppose we have a person's face here

07:22:26.240 --> 07:22:28.600
just at the corner of the image.

07:22:28.600 --> 07:22:31.680
You'll see that unlike with this image

07:22:31.680 --> 07:22:33.080
where this person was centered

07:22:33.080 --> 07:22:36.080
and that our kennel was able,

07:22:36.080 --> 07:22:40.560
our filter was able to pass through each and every part

07:22:40.560 --> 07:22:41.840
of this image.

07:22:41.840 --> 07:22:45.440
With this one, we have a different scenario.

07:22:46.560 --> 07:22:47.680
We've just modified this

07:22:47.680 --> 07:22:49.960
so it looks similar to what we had here.

07:22:49.960 --> 07:22:52.840
Now, what we could do is

07:22:54.080 --> 07:22:56.960
if we monitor the number of times

07:22:56.960 --> 07:22:59.880
this filter goes through the person's head,

07:22:59.880 --> 07:23:03.400
we would see that we'll have here one time

07:23:03.400 --> 07:23:06.280
because our filter passes here once.

07:23:06.280 --> 07:23:10.160
The next time after the sliding, we'll have this.

07:23:10.160 --> 07:23:13.680
So second time, the next time this other sliding

07:23:13.680 --> 07:23:16.320
will lead to this four times.

07:23:16.320 --> 07:23:17.840
So at least it passes through the head

07:23:17.840 --> 07:23:20.840
and then here we have five times.

07:23:20.840 --> 07:23:23.620
Now we have one, two, three, four times actually.

07:23:23.620 --> 07:23:26.840
But in this case, since we are on the borders,

07:23:26.840 --> 07:23:30.180
you'll see we have this one time

07:23:30.180 --> 07:23:31.800
and that's practically all.

07:23:31.800 --> 07:23:35.040
So we see that in this example or in this case

07:23:35.040 --> 07:23:36.720
where we are on the borders,

07:23:36.720 --> 07:23:39.280
this influences the outputs

07:23:40.640 --> 07:23:45.640
in a smaller way or exerts less influence

07:23:45.920 --> 07:23:48.620
on the values we get in this feature maps

07:23:48.620 --> 07:23:50.300
which are generated.

07:23:50.300 --> 07:23:52.240
So in an example where this,

07:23:52.240 --> 07:23:54.960
let's suppose an example where all this wasn't there

07:23:54.960 --> 07:23:58.720
and that where our information lies most is this,

07:23:58.720 --> 07:24:03.560
you will find that it would have been better to at least

07:24:03.560 --> 07:24:05.640
pass through this head region

07:24:05.640 --> 07:24:08.100
just as we did with this year

07:24:08.100 --> 07:24:10.280
where we pass through the head region four times

07:24:10.280 --> 07:24:13.260
and we're able to extract very useful information

07:24:13.260 --> 07:24:16.440
from this image because our filter

07:24:16.440 --> 07:24:19.160
goes through this many more times.

07:24:19.160 --> 07:24:22.040
Now to remedy this situation, we have the pattern.

07:24:22.040 --> 07:24:23.680
You see that when we increase the pattern,

07:24:23.680 --> 07:24:26.780
so let's take this to one, we've increased the pattern,

07:24:26.780 --> 07:24:28.840
let's increase the pattern to,

07:24:28.840 --> 07:24:30.760
oh, okay, yeah, let's fix to just one.

07:24:30.760 --> 07:24:33.120
Anyway, we just increase the pattern to one

07:24:33.120 --> 07:24:35.220
and then we'll retake that example.

07:24:36.140 --> 07:24:38.080
Now we're taking the example,

07:24:38.080 --> 07:24:41.420
we'll see that if we maintain our filter size of two,

07:24:41.420 --> 07:24:45.520
this filter here will at least touch the head

07:24:45.520 --> 07:24:50.440
although slightly and then the second one does this twice.

07:24:50.440 --> 07:24:54.520
So in this case, our filter touches the head twice

07:24:54.520 --> 07:24:56.840
as compared to previously where it touches the head

07:24:56.840 --> 07:25:01.280
only once and hence this useful information

07:25:01.280 --> 07:25:05.320
has more influence on the output features

07:25:05.320 --> 07:25:07.600
which are being generated right here

07:25:07.600 --> 07:25:10.320
and which is very important as practically

07:25:10.320 --> 07:25:11.340
that's what we're trying to do.

07:25:11.340 --> 07:25:14.820
We're trying to extract information from this input

07:25:14.820 --> 07:25:16.320
and pass to the output.

07:25:17.580 --> 07:25:21.600
Another hyper parameter which we could look at is a stride.

07:25:21.600 --> 07:25:24.480
Now note that for now we've looked at this one

07:25:24.480 --> 07:25:25.880
this and this.

07:25:25.880 --> 07:25:28.460
So here's what we call the hyper parameters.

07:25:29.360 --> 07:25:31.760
We have the stride and we'll understand

07:25:31.760 --> 07:25:33.320
surely how this works.

07:25:33.320 --> 07:25:35.280
For now we'll deal with a stride of one.

07:25:35.280 --> 07:25:36.200
Why one?

07:25:36.200 --> 07:25:39.720
Simply because you just slide in through one step

07:25:39.720 --> 07:25:40.800
we're going to the next.

07:25:40.800 --> 07:25:44.760
So you just slide one, one, one and so on and so forth.

07:25:44.760 --> 07:25:46.480
Now if we move this to two,

07:25:46.480 --> 07:25:50.160
we start from this position right here, fix that.

07:25:50.160 --> 07:25:51.600
You start from this position.

07:25:51.600 --> 07:25:54.320
There's a problem.

07:25:55.360 --> 07:25:58.880
You get back to one and back to two.

07:25:58.880 --> 07:26:01.520
You notice that as we go to one this turns blue

07:26:01.520 --> 07:26:03.440
and then to two it turns red.

07:26:03.440 --> 07:26:05.720
The reason why it turns red is because

07:26:05.720 --> 07:26:10.160
there is no valid output or there is no whole number

07:26:10.160 --> 07:26:14.520
which with a stride of two can produce an output.

07:26:14.520 --> 07:26:16.120
So in fact what we're going to do

07:26:16.120 --> 07:26:18.600
is we modify this input right here.

07:26:18.600 --> 07:26:20.700
So modify this input, this works now.

07:26:20.700 --> 07:26:22.920
So it's possible for us to leave from this input of six

07:26:22.920 --> 07:26:25.400
to this output, but with a stride of two.

07:26:25.400 --> 07:26:27.480
So let's now understand how strides work.

07:26:27.480 --> 07:26:28.760
We start with this.

07:26:28.760 --> 07:26:31.080
Notice now how we're going to skip two steps

07:26:31.080 --> 07:26:32.360
instead of just one step.

07:26:32.360 --> 07:26:34.980
So as we go, you see we skip two steps.

07:26:34.980 --> 07:26:39.040
Notice two, two and that's it.

07:26:39.040 --> 07:26:40.000
So we move to the next.

07:26:40.000 --> 07:26:42.280
And now even moving downward, you see,

07:26:42.280 --> 07:26:43.760
it's not like with a stride one

07:26:43.760 --> 07:26:46.440
where we just had one step, like with a stride one

07:26:46.440 --> 07:26:50.120
we just did this one step, but with a stride two

07:26:50.120 --> 07:26:52.320
we're going two steps below.

07:26:52.320 --> 07:26:53.360
So that's it.

07:26:53.360 --> 07:26:58.360
Now that said, increasing the size of our stride

07:26:58.920 --> 07:27:03.080
actually reduces the size of our output

07:27:03.080 --> 07:27:06.000
and hence reduces the amount of information

07:27:06.000 --> 07:27:08.360
we extract from the inputs.

07:27:09.400 --> 07:27:11.760
And so in general, we get better results

07:27:11.760 --> 07:27:15.160
by working with smaller kernels and smaller stride values

07:27:15.160 --> 07:27:18.640
since we're able to extract more information

07:27:18.640 --> 07:27:19.940
from our inputs.

07:27:20.820 --> 07:27:23.840
Generally the kernel size is used to read

07:27:23.840 --> 07:27:26.560
the kernel size of three is generally used in practice

07:27:26.560 --> 07:27:28.440
and a stride of one.

07:27:28.440 --> 07:27:31.580
So we may decide to use pattern or not.

07:27:33.080 --> 07:27:36.720
And the new formula when we include the pattern

07:27:36.720 --> 07:27:39.520
and the stride is given as such.

07:27:39.520 --> 07:27:43.600
So we have the output equal to input size

07:27:43.600 --> 07:27:47.560
minus the field of size plus two times the pattern

07:27:47.560 --> 07:27:52.340
divided by the stride plus one.

07:27:52.340 --> 07:27:55.360
In this case, if we have an input size of six,

07:27:55.360 --> 07:27:59.840
so here we have six, we have six minus field of size three

07:28:00.880 --> 07:28:04.200
plus two times the pattern, also our pattern one

07:28:04.200 --> 07:28:08.360
so it's plus two, divide that by the stride,

07:28:08.360 --> 07:28:11.640
stride is one and plus one.

07:28:11.640 --> 07:28:14.940
There we go, we have an answer of three plus two,

07:28:14.940 --> 07:28:17.440
which is five and then plus one, which is six.

07:28:17.440 --> 07:28:20.780
So that's how we obtain this output size right.

07:28:21.680 --> 07:28:24.880
Also note that one good thing when working with a library

07:28:24.880 --> 07:28:29.560
like TensorFlow is when you don't know the exact pattern

07:28:29.560 --> 07:28:34.060
to use such that you have particular output size,

07:28:34.060 --> 07:28:36.820
you could specify the pattern to be valid.

07:28:36.820 --> 07:28:38.840
Once you specify the pattern to be valid,

07:28:38.840 --> 07:28:42.360
TensorFlow automatically calculates the pattern for you

07:28:42.360 --> 07:28:46.760
such that the output you want matches up.

07:28:46.760 --> 07:28:50.440
Up to this point, we've been supposing that our input image

07:28:50.440 --> 07:28:55.140
is two dimensional, that is we have a height and a width.

07:28:55.140 --> 07:28:59.920
Now what if we use the kinds of images we have in real life,

07:28:59.920 --> 07:29:04.920
that is 3D images where we have the red channel,

07:29:05.480 --> 07:29:09.360
the green channel and the blue channel.

07:29:09.360 --> 07:29:14.000
So if we have this RGB image right here, RGB image,

07:29:14.000 --> 07:29:17.320
we'll see how we get the output.

07:29:17.320 --> 07:29:20.660
Now the way this is done is quite straightforward.

07:29:20.660 --> 07:29:23.680
So what we have is in this case, we include this pattern.

07:29:23.680 --> 07:29:26.760
So you see the zero pattern, we've included this pattern

07:29:26.760 --> 07:29:30.040
and then we have our kennel right here.

07:29:30.040 --> 07:29:32.700
Now notice how we have this kennel

07:29:32.700 --> 07:29:36.560
and then it goes through this first part

07:29:36.560 --> 07:29:38.680
or this top left corner.

07:29:38.680 --> 07:29:42.300
And what we do is we multiply each value right here.

07:29:42.300 --> 07:29:44.640
We have negative one times zero

07:29:44.640 --> 07:29:48.240
plus negative one times zero plus negative one times zero

07:29:48.240 --> 07:29:51.560
and so on and so forth up to plus negative one times zero

07:29:51.560 --> 07:29:53.900
right here, you have negative one times four.

07:29:53.900 --> 07:29:55.600
So basically what we're doing here

07:29:55.600 --> 07:29:57.320
is kind of like a dot product.

07:29:57.320 --> 07:29:59.240
So we're taking all these elements,

07:29:59.240 --> 07:30:00.800
multiplying by the corresponding elements

07:30:00.800 --> 07:30:03.000
and then adding all this up.

07:30:03.000 --> 07:30:06.220
Once all this added up, we should have 41.

07:30:06.220 --> 07:30:07.960
So you could take this as a simple exercise

07:30:07.960 --> 07:30:10.160
and then you should be able to get 41.

07:30:10.160 --> 07:30:12.720
And then we have this other kennel right here

07:30:12.720 --> 07:30:14.700
where we repeat the same process.

07:30:14.700 --> 07:30:19.100
We have this and then we have this kennel, we have this.

07:30:19.100 --> 07:30:22.800
So here what we do is we have, we take this,

07:30:22.800 --> 07:30:25.480
we obtain this value, take this obtain this value,

07:30:25.480 --> 07:30:28.560
this obtain this value and then we add all this up

07:30:28.560 --> 07:30:30.420
to get this answer 41.

07:30:30.420 --> 07:30:32.160
Now note that we also have the bias

