conv, pull, and softmax.

07:49:14.920 --> 07:49:17.880
So here we have this on Amnes dataset.

07:49:17.880 --> 07:49:20.840
And then we have in this passed in, as you could see,

07:49:20.840 --> 07:49:23.860
notice how in this first layers,

07:49:23.860 --> 07:49:28.860
the feature maps actually contain much more visual content

07:49:29.240 --> 07:49:33.560
as compared to this final layers right here.

07:49:33.560 --> 07:49:36.120
See, if you look at this final layers,

07:49:36.120 --> 07:49:39.520
you see that we have this feature maps,

07:49:39.520 --> 07:49:42.900
which instead contain content,

07:49:42.900 --> 07:49:47.120
which permits us see whether a particular input

07:49:47.120 --> 07:49:49.880
belongs to a particular class.

07:49:49.880 --> 07:49:53.680
If you want to build convolutional layers with TensorFlow,

07:49:53.680 --> 07:49:58.080
you could make use of this TensorFlow Keras model right here.

07:49:58.080 --> 07:50:01.460
So that said, we're just gonna come to the layers,

07:50:01.460 --> 07:50:04.140
see other Keras, and then we pick layers,

07:50:04.140 --> 07:50:07.800
and then we'll find the conv2D layer.

07:50:07.800 --> 07:50:09.840
So here is the layer we're searching for,

07:50:09.840 --> 07:50:13.160
double click on that, and then here we go.

07:50:13.160 --> 07:50:18.160
We have the arguments, that's what we pass in this layer.

07:50:18.240 --> 07:50:20.480
The filters right here corresponds

07:50:20.480 --> 07:50:22.000
to the number of filters.

07:50:22.000 --> 07:50:23.300
So let's take this off.

07:50:23.300 --> 07:50:25.760
Here is the number of filters, we've seen this already,

07:50:25.760 --> 07:50:28.840
and F, and then the kernel size

07:50:28.840 --> 07:50:32.000
corresponds to the filter size.

07:50:32.000 --> 07:50:33.980
From there, we move to the stripes,

07:50:33.980 --> 07:50:38.980
which take a tuple, let's take this off and check this out.

07:50:39.680 --> 07:50:42.720
So here we have this tuple actually,

07:50:42.720 --> 07:50:46.800
and then what's important to note here is the fact that

07:50:46.800 --> 07:50:50.640
if you want to do a striding of say one two,

07:50:50.640 --> 07:50:55.640
that is one two, then in the height dimension,

07:50:57.960 --> 07:51:00.120
your striding is gonna be equal one,

07:51:00.120 --> 07:51:03.640
and then the width dimension is trying to be equal two.

07:51:03.640 --> 07:51:06.560
So if we have this feature map right here,

07:51:06.560 --> 07:51:10.680
and we pass a kernel, we are gonna be sliding

07:51:12.560 --> 07:51:16.600
and skipping two steps in this horizontal direction,

07:51:16.600 --> 07:51:19.840
while when we come in, when we're going downwards,

07:51:19.840 --> 07:51:22.240
we are gonna be skipping only one step.

07:51:22.240 --> 07:51:23.600
So that's what this means.

07:51:23.600 --> 07:51:26.840
Now in the case where we are having exactly the same

07:51:26.840 --> 07:51:30.120
number of steps sliding horizontally and vertically,

07:51:30.120 --> 07:51:32.880
then the striding, like in this case,

07:51:32.880 --> 07:51:34.940
can just be given as equal one.

07:51:36.080 --> 07:51:37.640
From there we have the pattern,

07:51:37.640 --> 07:51:39.560
by default the pattern is valid.

07:51:39.560 --> 07:51:43.980
Here we tell that for the pattern,

07:51:43.980 --> 07:51:45.640
if the pattern is valid,

07:51:45.640 --> 07:51:47.320
it means there's actually no pattern,

07:51:47.320 --> 07:51:48.880
so we have zero pattern.

07:51:48.880 --> 07:51:50.600
And then when the pattern is same,

07:51:50.600 --> 07:51:52.880
the result of the pattern are the,

07:51:52.880 --> 07:51:56.360
we've padded with zeros to ensure that

07:51:56.360 --> 07:51:59.660
the input dimension equal the output dimension.

07:51:59.660 --> 07:52:02.920
Also note that this is only possible

07:52:02.920 --> 07:52:05.600
when the stride is equal one.

07:52:05.600 --> 07:52:09.800
So that said, oh, if we have a feature map like this,

07:52:09.800 --> 07:52:13.640
let's say we have 60 by 60.

07:52:13.640 --> 07:52:16.640
Then we wanna pass this to a conv layer

07:52:16.640 --> 07:52:19.000
to get an output feature map.

07:52:19.000 --> 07:52:21.000
And if we want this output feature map

07:52:21.000 --> 07:52:25.440
to be the same dimension as the input that is 60 by 60,

07:52:25.440 --> 07:52:27.760
then all we need to do is specify

07:52:27.760 --> 07:52:29.800
that the pattern is same.

07:52:29.800 --> 07:52:31.200
So once we have this,

07:52:31.200 --> 07:52:33.720
the output will be the same as that of the input.

07:52:34.720 --> 07:52:37.020
From here we have the data format.

07:52:37.020 --> 07:52:40.720
Now note that there are generally two kinds of data formats.

07:52:40.720 --> 07:52:43.080
That is we have height.

07:52:43.080 --> 07:52:46.200
So for an image, we have the height, width,

07:52:46.200 --> 07:52:48.680
and then the channel.

07:52:48.680 --> 07:52:53.680
So if we have a 224 by 224 by two V image,

07:52:59.360 --> 07:53:03.120
then we actually making use of this data format.

07:53:03.120 --> 07:53:05.560
Now we could also use this format

07:53:05.560 --> 07:53:06.800
where we start with a channel.

07:53:06.800 --> 07:53:11.800
So we have the channel by height and by width.

07:53:13.600 --> 07:53:17.200
And so here we have three by 224 by 224.

07:53:17.200 --> 07:53:20.160
Note that by default we have the channel last.

07:53:20.160 --> 07:53:23.720
So by default we have this first convention last right here.

07:53:23.720 --> 07:53:27.320
But if you wanna take this convention,

07:53:27.320 --> 07:53:28.880
all you need to do is to specify

07:53:28.880 --> 07:53:31.840
that you're working with the channels first.

07:53:33.160 --> 07:53:35.800
Then from here we have the dilation rate.

07:53:35.800 --> 07:53:40.200
So we'll look at this GitHub repository by Vido Mulung

07:53:41.720 --> 07:53:45.580
where he uses animations to explain how convolutions work.

07:53:45.580 --> 07:53:48.120
So you could scroll down

07:53:48.120 --> 07:53:51.680
and you would find the dilated convolution animation,

07:53:51.680 --> 07:53:54.340
which when we click on, we have this.

07:53:54.340 --> 07:53:55.640
So there we go.

07:53:55.640 --> 07:53:57.520
We have this example in which

07:53:57.520 --> 07:54:01.480
the dilation rate is equal to.

07:54:01.480 --> 07:54:06.000
So the way this dilation works is we have this kernel

07:54:06.000 --> 07:54:10.600
which initially had no spaces between its values.

07:54:10.600 --> 07:54:12.480
So we had something like this.

07:54:12.480 --> 07:54:15.600
We had this three by three kernel.

07:54:15.600 --> 07:54:18.840
And now what we do is we have this spaces

07:54:18.840 --> 07:54:23.360
or this holds between these different values.

07:54:23.360 --> 07:54:25.600
And so now what we get is

07:54:25.600 --> 07:54:29.640
some sort of five by five kernel right here.

07:54:29.640 --> 07:54:33.080
If we're working with a dilation rate r equals three,

07:54:33.080 --> 07:54:36.600
then instead of a single whole year,

07:54:36.600 --> 07:54:39.560
we are gonna fit in two other holes.

07:54:39.560 --> 07:54:42.960
So then I have this, we just have one, two,

07:54:42.960 --> 07:54:44.520
and then we fit this.

07:54:44.520 --> 07:54:48.000
Then we have one, two, and then we fit this.

07:54:48.000 --> 07:54:50.760
So that's how we build this out.

07:54:50.760 --> 07:54:53.800
We have this and then that.

07:54:53.800 --> 07:54:57.360
Now to get the shape, we have one, two, three, four,

07:54:57.360 --> 07:54:58.640
five, six, seven.

07:54:58.640 --> 07:55:03.320
So we have a seven by seven filter now.

07:55:03.320 --> 07:55:06.440
It should be noted that the dilated convolutions

07:55:06.440 --> 07:55:10.800
are used in problems where we wanna keep increasing

07:55:10.800 --> 07:55:15.800
the receptive field as we keep going deeper in the network

07:55:16.480 --> 07:55:19.700
while maintaining the number of parameters.

07:55:19.700 --> 07:55:22.200
The next argument is the groups.

07:55:22.200 --> 07:55:24.560
And according to the documentation,

07:55:24.560 --> 07:55:27.520
this is a positive integer specifying the number of groups

07:55:27.520 --> 07:55:31.200
in which the input is split along the channel axis.

07:55:31.200 --> 07:55:33.560
This means that in this case, for example,

07:55:33.560 --> 07:55:36.680
we could break this up into three different groups.

07:55:36.680 --> 07:55:41.200
So we have one, two, and then three groups.

07:55:41.200 --> 07:55:45.280
Then each group has its own group filters.

07:55:45.280 --> 07:55:47.440
And then the output is a concatenation

07:55:47.440 --> 07:55:50.680
of all the group results across the channel axis.

07:55:51.680 --> 07:55:56.080
From here, we have the activation we've seen already.

07:55:56.080 --> 07:56:00.280
And then we'll see whether we're gonna use the bias or not.

07:56:00.280 --> 07:56:04.200
We have this kernel that is the weight initialization.

07:56:04.200 --> 07:56:06.680
And then we have the bias initialization.

07:56:06.680 --> 07:56:11.080
We have this regularizes, which we'll see subsequently.

07:56:11.080 --> 07:56:14.860
And then we have the kernel and bias constraints.

07:56:19.480 --> 07:56:23.040
In order to create our convolutional neural network,

07:56:23.040 --> 07:56:26.280
we're just gonna make use of this sequential API

07:56:26.280 --> 07:56:28.900
which we had built previously.

07:56:28.900 --> 07:56:31.560
And then we'll define the input layer.

07:56:31.560 --> 07:56:34.640
So here we have this input shape 224.

