other than the accuracy, which we've seen so far.

09:37:38.440 --> 09:37:43.440
To better understand why working with other accuracy

09:37:43.440 --> 09:37:45.400
isn't always a great idea,

09:37:45.400 --> 09:37:47.720
we have to take into consideration the fact that

09:37:48.720 --> 09:37:53.720
our model on a test set has 94% accuracy.

09:37:55.240 --> 09:37:57.600
Now, this means that we have

09:37:59.200 --> 09:38:01.880
six out of 100

09:38:03.400 --> 09:38:06.120
predictions which are actually false.

09:38:07.640 --> 09:38:10.320
Now, what if I get to the hospital

09:38:10.320 --> 09:38:14.240
and I'm told that I don't have malaria

09:38:14.240 --> 09:38:17.560
when in fact I actually have this disease?

09:38:18.600 --> 09:38:22.160
So that said, the model predicts uninfected

09:38:22.160 --> 09:38:27.160
and I actually have the parasite in my bloodstream.

09:38:27.480 --> 09:38:31.000
Now, this particular situation becomes very dangerous

09:38:31.000 --> 09:38:34.640
because the patient gets by home thinking

09:38:34.640 --> 09:38:36.720
he or she doesn't need any treatment,

09:38:36.720 --> 09:38:41.360
whereas that patient actually has this parasite.

09:38:41.360 --> 09:38:45.920
You see that even with a 94% accuracy,

09:38:45.920 --> 09:38:47.800
we wouldn't be able to save ourselves

09:38:47.800 --> 09:38:51.680
from such chaotic model predictions.

09:38:51.680 --> 09:38:55.000
Now, in another example, you have a situation

09:38:55.000 --> 09:38:58.760
where the actual is, let's put it out here.

09:38:58.760 --> 09:39:00.840
So in another example, we can have a situation

09:39:00.840 --> 09:39:03.440
where the actual is unparasitized.

09:39:03.440 --> 09:39:07.480
So actually you do not have this parasite,

09:39:07.480 --> 09:39:11.440
but the model predicts that you have the parasite.

09:39:11.440 --> 09:39:16.440
Now, in this case, although we have a wrong prediction,

09:39:17.000 --> 09:39:20.480
we have actually a less chaotic situation

09:39:20.480 --> 09:39:23.400
as compared to this previous case here,

09:39:23.400 --> 09:39:28.400
since at least actually you are uninfected.

09:39:28.400 --> 09:39:33.400
If we consider negative, that's this negative

09:39:33.760 --> 09:39:38.760
to be uninfected and positive

09:39:39.320 --> 09:39:44.080
to contain the parasite, that's P.

09:39:44.080 --> 09:39:46.760
So here we consider we have positive

09:39:46.760 --> 09:39:48.160
and then we have negative.

09:39:48.160 --> 09:39:50.440
So here we have negative, uninfected

09:39:50.440 --> 09:39:53.320
and positive parasitized.

09:39:53.320 --> 09:39:56.680
Now, if we let this, you will find that this first situation

09:39:56.680 --> 09:40:00.160
where actually we have the parasite

09:40:00.160 --> 09:40:02.760
and the model predicts unparasitized

09:40:02.760 --> 09:40:07.760
is known as a false negative.

09:40:08.520 --> 09:40:10.880
So here we have this false negative.

09:40:13.480 --> 09:40:16.920
And this is because the model predicts negative

09:40:16.920 --> 09:40:19.920
when it isn't actually negative.

09:40:19.920 --> 09:40:24.840
So since we have this wrong prediction for negative,

09:40:24.840 --> 09:40:27.040
we call it a false negative.

09:40:27.040 --> 09:40:30.880
And in this case where we have the model predicting

09:40:31.800 --> 09:40:35.600
parasitized, that is positive, when it's actually negative,

09:40:35.600 --> 09:40:38.720
we call this a false positive.

09:40:38.720 --> 09:40:43.720
So here we have FP and here we have FN.

09:40:44.880 --> 09:40:47.400
Now, there are two other scenarios

09:40:47.400 --> 09:40:51.840
that is with TN and TP.

09:40:51.840 --> 09:40:55.400
For the TN, we have the true negatives

09:40:55.400 --> 09:40:57.600
and the TP, the true positives.

09:40:57.600 --> 09:41:02.440
For the TN, we have the model predicting negative

09:41:02.440 --> 09:41:04.880
when actually we are negative.

09:41:04.880 --> 09:41:08.080
That is, we have the model saying this is uninfected

09:41:08.080 --> 09:41:10.000
when actually it's uninfected.

09:41:10.000 --> 09:41:11.360
So that's a true negative.

09:41:11.360 --> 09:41:13.240
And then for the true positive,

09:41:13.240 --> 09:41:17.320
the model predicts a positive that's parasitized

09:41:17.320 --> 09:41:19.120
when actually it's parasitized.

09:41:19.120 --> 09:41:21.280
So that's a true positive.

09:41:21.280 --> 09:41:23.600
Now, hopefully you've understood the concepts

09:41:23.600 --> 09:41:27.080
of true negatives, true positives, false negatives,

09:41:27.080 --> 09:41:29.240
and false positives.

09:41:30.440 --> 09:41:33.560
We can then summarize all this information

09:41:33.560 --> 09:41:37.240
in this matrix known as the confusion matrix.

09:41:37.240 --> 09:41:39.640
This confusion matrix, we have the true negatives,

09:41:39.640 --> 09:41:41.760
the number of true negatives here,

09:41:41.760 --> 09:41:43.800
the number of true positives,

09:41:43.800 --> 09:41:45.640
the number of false negatives,

09:41:45.640 --> 09:41:48.640
and the number of false positives.

09:41:48.640 --> 09:41:52.520
This means that if we have a test set of say,

09:41:52.520 --> 09:41:57.520
2,750 different data points,

09:41:57.720 --> 09:42:01.960
and then we run this or we evaluate this with our model,

09:42:01.960 --> 09:42:05.000
we'll be able to get this number of true negatives,

09:42:05.000 --> 09:42:06.720
get this number of false negatives,

09:42:06.720 --> 09:42:10.000
number of true positives, number of false positives,

09:42:10.000 --> 09:42:13.800
and hence better evaluate this model.

09:42:14.680 --> 09:42:18.080
So if we take this example where we've evaluated our model

09:42:18.080 --> 09:42:21.840
on the test set, and then this model A produces

09:42:21.840 --> 09:42:25.080
this confusion matrix, and this model B produces

09:42:25.080 --> 09:42:27.960
other confusion matrix, where here we see we have

09:42:27.960 --> 09:42:29.720
for the true negatives and true positives,

09:42:29.720 --> 09:42:33.760
we have 1,000, 1,000, that's 2,000 correctly

09:42:33.760 --> 09:42:37.920
predicted data points, and then here we have 1,000,

09:42:37.920 --> 09:42:40.760
1,000, 2,000 correctly predicted data points.

09:42:40.760 --> 09:42:45.760
And then for this model A, we have 700 false positives.

09:42:45.760 --> 09:42:50.760
And 50 false negatives.

09:42:51.440 --> 09:42:55.280
Whereas for model B, we have 50 false positives

09:42:55.280 --> 09:42:58.840
and 700 false negatives.

09:42:58.840 --> 09:43:01.680
Recall we had defined negative to be uninfected

09:43:01.680 --> 09:43:03.840
and positive to be parasitized.

09:43:04.720 --> 09:43:09.080
And hence, if we had to choose a model between A and B,

09:43:09.080 --> 09:43:13.040
we'll try to choose that model which minimizes

09:43:13.040 --> 09:43:15.960
the number of false negatives.

09:43:15.960 --> 09:43:20.840
So we are not saying that we shouldn't minimize

09:43:20.840 --> 09:43:23.160
the number of false positives because we have to try

09:43:23.160 --> 09:43:27.200
to minimize all the false predictions.

09:43:27.200 --> 09:43:29.760
But then, since with the false negatives,

09:43:30.640 --> 09:43:34.960
we are telling a sick person that he or she isn't sick,

09:43:36.520 --> 09:43:41.160
this at least is worse than telling a healthy person

09:43:41.160 --> 09:43:43.440
that he or she is sick.

09:43:43.440 --> 09:43:46.200
And so we'll try to prioritize the number

09:43:46.200 --> 09:43:50.440
of false negatives and based on this prioritization,

09:43:50.440 --> 09:43:55.440
we are gonna prefer model A since we have the smaller

09:43:55.640 --> 09:43:57.640
number of false negatives.

09:43:57.640 --> 09:44:02.000
And so here, we'll choose model A over model B.

09:44:02.920 --> 09:44:05.640
Now, as a quick note, you may decide to say

09:44:05.640 --> 09:44:10.640
no negative is for parasitized and positive is for uninfected.

09:44:10.640 --> 09:44:15.640
It isn't a must that this must be tied together like this,

09:44:16.480 --> 09:44:20.080
but for clarity purposes, it's better to look at it this way

09:44:20.080 --> 09:44:23.680
since saying you are tested negative means you're uninfected

09:44:23.680 --> 09:44:26.560
and tested positive means you're parasitized.

09:44:28.160 --> 09:44:31.080
Now, you should also note that depending on the kind

09:44:31.080 --> 09:44:34.040
of problem you want to solve, in some cases,

09:44:34.040 --> 09:44:37.480
you will want to prioritize minimizing the number

09:44:37.480 --> 09:44:41.720
of false positive over the number of false negatives.

09:44:41.720 --> 09:44:44.640
So this actually depends on the problem you're trying

09:44:44.640 --> 09:44:45.480
to solve.

09:44:46.600 --> 09:44:50.160
But in this case, we are prioritizing the number

09:44:50.160 --> 09:44:51.840
of false negatives.

09:44:51.840 --> 09:44:54.360
Now, based on what we've seen so far,

09:44:54.360 --> 09:44:59.320
we're gonna introduce several new performance metrics.

09:44:59.320 --> 09:45:02.960
And others take up this different formulas

09:45:02.960 --> 09:45:04.680
we could see right here.

09:45:04.680 --> 09:45:08.040
We have the precision, which is the number of true positives

09:45:08.040 --> 09:45:10.120
divided by the number of true positives,

09:45:10.120 --> 09:45:11.800
plus the number of false positives.

09:45:11.800 --> 09:45:15.120
Recall, true positives divided by a number

09:45:15.120 --> 09:45:17.960
of true positives plus a number of false negatives.

09:45:17.960 --> 09:45:20.880
The accuracy, the number of two negatives,

09:45:20.880 --> 09:45:23.060
plus number of true positives divided by number

09:45:23.060 --> 09:45:25.240
of true negatives plus true positives

09:45:25.240 --> 09:45:27.960
plus false negatives, plus false positives.

09:45:27.960 --> 09:45:32.280
So we'll stop in this first three for now.

09:45:32.280 --> 09:45:33.560
Now, what do you notice?

09:45:33.560 --> 09:45:36.340
You'll notice that in this position and recall,

09:45:36.340 --> 09:45:39.300
we have this true positive, true positive,

09:45:39.300 --> 09:45:42.060
and here true positive, true positive.

09:45:42.060 --> 09:45:44.260
What differentiates them is the fact that

09:45:44.260 --> 09:45:47.980
in the position we have false positive in the denominator,

09:45:47.980 --> 09:45:51.620
and in the recall we have false negative in the denominator.

09:45:51.620 --> 09:45:56.620
This means that if the number of false negatives is high,

09:45:56.860 --> 09:46:00.100
that is we have, let's say we have a constant,

09:46:00.100 --> 09:46:05.100
a constant K divided by a high value.

09:46:05.740 --> 09:46:08.220
So constant divided by a high value.

09:46:08.220 --> 09:46:10.020
So constant divided by high.

09:46:10.020 --> 09:46:12.860
Here we're going to have a low output.

09:46:12.860 --> 09:46:16.220
And so if we want to have a low recall,

09:46:16.220 --> 09:46:20.500
then we need to have a high number of false negatives.

09:46:20.500 --> 09:46:23.660
And if we want to have a low position,

09:46:23.660 --> 09:46:28.140
then we need to have a high number of false positives.

09:46:28.140 --> 09:46:30.660
Now in our case, we're trying to minimize

09:46:30.660 --> 09:46:34.220
the number of false negatives.

09:46:34.220 --> 09:46:35.420
And since we're trying to minimize

09:46:35.420 --> 09:46:36.800
the number of false negatives,

09:46:36.800 --> 09:46:41.700
it means that we're trying to maximize the recall.

09:46:41.700 --> 09:46:43.820
Since minimizing this denominator,

09:46:43.820 --> 09:46:48.820
we're until maximizing this overall TP on TP plus FN.

09:46:50.540 --> 09:46:53.340
And so here we're trying to prioritize

09:46:53.340 --> 09:46:56.240
the recall over the position.

09:46:56.240 --> 09:46:58.760
Now, if you look at the accuracy,

09:46:58.760 --> 09:47:02.040
you'll notice that we have TN plus TP

09:47:02.040 --> 09:47:04.060
and TN plus TP right here.

09:47:04.060 --> 09:47:08.560
TN plus TP, TN plus TP, and we have FN plus FP.

09:47:08.560 --> 09:47:11.800
If you are keen enough, you should see that

09:47:11.800 --> 09:47:16.040
this accuracy doesn't give any parity

09:47:16.040 --> 09:47:20.720
for whether the false negatives are the false positives.

09:47:20.720 --> 09:47:23.320
It treats these two as the same.

09:47:23.320 --> 09:47:26.520
But as we've seen previously in the real world,

09:47:26.520 --> 09:47:28.760
in solving real world problems,

09:47:28.760 --> 09:47:31.700
many times we'll have to prioritize.

09:47:31.700 --> 09:47:34.760
Hence, the accuracy may not always

09:47:34.760 --> 09:47:37.440
be the best metrics for our problem.

09:47:37.440 --> 09:47:41.720
In our case, we find that using the recall

09:47:43.640 --> 09:47:46.880
is even better than using the accuracy.

09:47:46.880 --> 09:47:51.800
As with the recall, we get to reduce or,

09:47:51.800 --> 09:47:53.720
with the recall, we get to see

09:47:53.720 --> 09:47:58.720
whether our model does well at minimizing

09:47:59.760 --> 09:48:01.640
the number of false negatives.

09:48:03.000 --> 09:48:05.320
Now, we also have this F1 score,

09:48:05.320 --> 09:48:07.280
two times the precision times recall,

09:48:07.280 --> 09:48:08.720
this precision is recall,

09:48:08.720 --> 09:48:10.880
divided by the precision plus recall,

09:48:10.880 --> 09:48:14.240
the specificity, the number of true negatives

09:48:14.240 --> 09:48:15.640
divided by number of true negatives

09:48:15.640 --> 09:48:17.460
plus number of false positives.

09:48:18.560 --> 09:48:21.720
And then we also have this ROC plot right here,

09:48:21.720 --> 09:48:25.960
ROC stands for receiver operating characteristics.

09:48:25.960 --> 09:48:27.580
Here we have the true positive rate

09:48:27.580 --> 09:48:29.840
and the false positive rate.

09:48:29.840 --> 09:48:33.160
The true positive rate is the number of true positives

09:48:33.160 --> 09:48:34.760
divided by number of true positives

09:48:34.760 --> 09:48:36.640
plus number of false negatives,

09:48:36.640 --> 09:48:39.120
which happens to be the recall.

09:48:39.120 --> 09:48:41.320
And then the false positive rate

09:48:41.320 --> 09:48:42.880
is the number of false positives

09:48:42.880 --> 09:48:44.960
divided by number of false positives

09:48:44.960 --> 09:48:47.620
plus the number of true negatives,

09:48:47.620 --> 09:48:50.560
which if you look carefully, you'll find that is equal.

09:48:50.560 --> 09:48:53.160
One minus the specificity,

09:48:53.160 --> 09:48:54.960
which has been defined right here.

09:48:55.960 --> 09:48:59.440
Before getting to understand this ROC plot,

09:48:59.440 --> 09:49:01.480
which we've put out here,

09:49:01.480 --> 09:49:05.340
let's recall this two models,

09:49:05.340 --> 09:49:07.320
which we had described previously,

09:49:07.320 --> 09:49:09.720
that's model A and model B,

09:49:09.720 --> 09:49:14.720
where model A had a smaller number of false negatives

09:49:15.760 --> 09:49:17.720
as compared to model B.

09:49:17.720 --> 09:49:22.720
Now, if we pick out just this model A

09:49:22.720 --> 09:49:26.520
and then we are interested in reducing this,

09:49:26.520 --> 09:49:28.040
or let's say we pick our model B.

09:49:28.040 --> 09:49:29.800
Suppose we pick our model B,

09:49:29.800 --> 09:49:33.040
I will interested in reducing this number

09:49:33.040 --> 09:49:35.240
of false negatives right here.

09:49:36.300 --> 09:49:41.300
Then one solution could be that of modifying the threshold.

09:49:42.240 --> 09:49:45.260
So if we have a threshold of 0.5,

09:49:45.260 --> 09:49:49.360
meaning that above, like we have the 0.5,

09:49:49.360 --> 09:49:52.440
below 0.5 we consider negative,

09:49:52.440 --> 09:49:54.600
above 0.5 we consider positive,

09:49:54.600 --> 09:49:57.820
that is pasteurized and then below uninfected.

09:49:58.760 --> 09:50:03.400
Then what I could do here is reduce the threshold.

09:50:03.400 --> 09:50:07.120
So if I take the threshold to say value of 0.2,

09:50:07.120 --> 09:50:08.780
let's take this here.

09:50:08.780 --> 09:50:11.960
So I've reduced the threshold now to 0.2.

09:50:11.960 --> 09:50:16.320
You see that for most of the predictions,

09:50:16.320 --> 09:50:18.720
our model is going to say that this

09:50:18.720 --> 09:50:22.740
is a parasitized output

09:50:22.740 --> 09:50:25.960
since now the threshold has been reduced.

09:50:25.960 --> 09:50:29.880
This means that if we have a model prediction of 0.3,

09:50:29.880 --> 09:50:33.960
which initially would have been uninfected,

09:50:33.960 --> 09:50:38.200
now this model sees this as parasitized.

09:50:38.200 --> 09:50:42.640
And so this makes it now more difficult

09:50:42.640 --> 09:50:46.360
for our model to have false negatives

09:50:46.360 --> 09:50:50.380
since our model now has this tendency

09:50:50.380 --> 09:50:55.380
of predicting that a given input image is parasitized.

09:50:57.860 --> 09:51:02.400
That said, we now need to look for a way

09:51:02.400 --> 09:51:05.240
that we could automate this process.

09:51:05.240 --> 09:51:08.360
That is we want to be able

09:51:08.360 --> 09:51:11.840
to choose this threshold correctly or rightly.

09:51:11.840 --> 09:51:16.840
Because if let's say we take a threshold of say 0.001,

09:51:18.640 --> 09:51:22.640
it means that anytime our model predicts less than 0.01,

09:51:22.640 --> 09:51:27.640
it's uninfected and then greater than 0.01 is parasitized,

09:51:29.640 --> 09:51:31.560
then this will be very dangerous

09:51:31.560 --> 09:51:34.340
for the overall model performance

09:51:34.340 --> 09:51:38.480
as now most times would have the model predicting

09:51:38.480 --> 09:51:41.820
that the input is parasitized.

09:51:43.080 --> 09:51:47.480
And so our aim here is to pick this threshold

09:51:47.480 --> 09:51:52.120
such that this number of true positives

09:51:52.120 --> 09:51:56.420
and true negatives we've had right here don't get reduced.

09:51:58.700 --> 09:52:01.240
Now, the way we could look at this

09:52:01.240 --> 09:52:04.260
is now by using this ROC plot.

09:52:04.260 --> 09:52:07.320
With this ROC plot, what you actually have here

09:52:07.320 --> 09:52:10.740
is the different true positive rates

09:52:10.740 --> 09:52:14.840
and false positive rates you'd have at a given threshold.

09:52:14.840 --> 09:52:17.560
So this means that a point picked here

09:52:17.560 --> 09:52:20.240
is just a given threshold.

09:52:20.240 --> 09:52:24.760
Now let's suppose that the threshold 0.5 is about here.

09:52:24.760 --> 09:52:25.840
So let's pick this.

09:52:25.840 --> 09:52:28.040
Let's suppose that this is 0.5.

09:52:28.040 --> 09:52:30.880
We could pick another threshold.

09:52:30.880 --> 09:52:35.880
Let's say this one is say 0.2 and so on and so forth.

09:52:36.160 --> 09:52:37.740
Let's say this one is 0.1.

09:52:40.240 --> 09:52:42.200
Now we could have another model

09:52:42.200 --> 09:52:44.160
with this different ROC plot,

09:52:44.160 --> 09:52:47.080
another one with this kind of ROC plot,

09:52:47.080 --> 09:52:50.440
but note that overall our aim is to ensure

09:52:50.440 --> 09:52:54.400
that this false positive rates is minimized

09:52:54.400 --> 09:52:57.080
and the true positive rate is maximized.

09:52:57.080 --> 09:53:00.360
So if we have an ROC plot, which is like this,

09:53:00.360 --> 09:53:03.600
so let's redraw this.

09:53:03.600 --> 09:53:05.880
If we have this kind of ROC plot,

