10:30:45.380 --> 10:30:50.380
After say this point, our models, even this point here,

10:30:52.100 --> 10:30:57.100
our model's validation accuracy doesn't increase any further.

10:30:57.460 --> 10:31:01.060
So what we have in here is something like this.

10:31:01.060 --> 10:31:05.980
We have this train accuracy which increases

10:31:05.980 --> 10:31:10.980
and goes towards one and then the validation accuracy

10:31:10.980 --> 10:31:15.600
which is something like this.

10:31:15.600 --> 10:31:18.180
Now in some other cases, you would even have situations

10:31:18.180 --> 10:31:20.760
where this starts to drop.

10:31:20.760 --> 10:31:23.960
Nonetheless, in this case, we have this plot

10:31:23.960 --> 10:31:26.860
where it just kind of like stabilizes

10:31:26.860 --> 10:31:29.000
and doesn't increase any further.

10:31:29.000 --> 10:31:34.000
Now note that this kind of situation is known as overfeeding.

10:31:34.000 --> 10:31:39.000
In overfeeding, the model starts to overfeed the training data.

10:31:42.840 --> 10:31:45.920
So because the model has been trained on the training data

10:31:45.920 --> 10:31:49.400
and not on the validation data, at certain points,

10:31:49.400 --> 10:31:53.120
the model stops or ceases to generalize

10:31:53.120 --> 10:31:56.860
because the aim of this training process

10:31:56.860 --> 10:31:59.480
is not to come up with a model

10:31:59.480 --> 10:32:01.820
which only performs well on the training data.

10:32:01.820 --> 10:32:03.600
We're trying to come up with a model

10:32:03.600 --> 10:32:06.840
which performs well on any type of data,

10:32:06.840 --> 10:32:10.120
be it a train, be it a validation or the test data.

10:32:10.120 --> 10:32:14.140
So if we're able to have a model which does the same

10:32:14.140 --> 10:32:16.860
or which has the same performance with a train

10:32:16.860 --> 10:32:18.940
and with a validation, with a test,

10:32:18.940 --> 10:32:21.200
then that model is an ideal one.

10:32:21.200 --> 10:32:24.420
But in this case, we see that as we keep on training,

10:32:25.440 --> 10:32:28.880
the model's parameters have been modified

10:32:28.880 --> 10:32:31.280
to suit only the training data.

10:32:31.280 --> 10:32:34.660
And this is very dangerous because at a certain point,

10:32:35.720 --> 10:32:38.240
you may feel like because you're having

10:32:38.240 --> 10:32:42.860
high training accuracy, your model's performing well.

10:32:42.860 --> 10:32:46.400
Whereas this isn't the case because when your model

10:32:46.400 --> 10:32:51.240
will be shown new data, like the validation in this case,

10:32:51.240 --> 10:32:55.920
and the test later on, this model wouldn't perform

10:32:55.920 --> 10:32:58.480
as well as it will do,

10:32:58.480 --> 10:33:01.280
or as well as it's doing with the training data.

10:33:01.280 --> 10:33:05.600
So to avoid this kind of false measurements,

10:33:05.600 --> 10:33:07.840
we tend to stop the training

10:33:07.840 --> 10:33:11.800
once this overfeeding status will occur.

10:33:11.800 --> 10:33:14.320
So this means that if you're training

10:33:14.320 --> 10:33:16.320
and then your validation, let's take,

10:33:16.320 --> 10:33:18.760
let's suppose we're stopping at this here.

10:33:18.760 --> 10:33:22.560
So we're training and then your validation data

10:33:22.560 --> 10:33:25.840
or your validation accuracy seems to be constant,

10:33:25.840 --> 10:33:30.000
whereas that of the training seems to kind of increase,

10:33:30.000 --> 10:33:34.460
then it's better for you to stop training at this point.

10:33:34.460 --> 10:33:38.240
Because after this point, the model parameters

10:33:38.240 --> 10:33:41.960
are just being modified to suit the training data

10:33:41.960 --> 10:33:45.640
and it doesn't really generalize, which is the case here

10:33:45.640 --> 10:33:50.480
because we're trying to extract some information

10:33:50.480 --> 10:33:53.840
from this data and make the model intelligent.

10:33:53.840 --> 10:33:56.600
So the model doesn't become intelligent

10:33:56.600 --> 10:34:01.520
by only modifying its weights or parameters

10:34:01.520 --> 10:34:03.880
based on the data it's been trained upon.

10:34:03.880 --> 10:34:07.080
It's intelligent because after being trained,

10:34:07.080 --> 10:34:12.080
it can perform well on data it has never, ever seen.

10:34:12.720 --> 10:34:15.920
So here we have this early stopping

10:34:15.920 --> 10:34:20.320
where after we notice that the validation accuracy

10:34:20.320 --> 10:34:22.520
doesn't seem to increase any further,

10:34:22.520 --> 10:34:25.060
we just kind of like stop the training

10:34:25.060 --> 10:34:27.480
and then use the model parameters

10:34:27.480 --> 10:34:29.240
from this number of epochs.

10:34:29.240 --> 10:34:32.620
So we could see that after say 12 epochs,

10:34:32.620 --> 10:34:34.520
we just stop the training.

10:34:34.520 --> 10:34:37.520
Now let's take this off and then do replicate

10:34:37.520 --> 10:34:39.080
something similar for the loss.

10:34:40.020 --> 10:34:42.800
So if we're having a loss,

10:34:42.800 --> 10:34:46.760
we could have something like this and then that.

10:34:46.760 --> 10:34:49.280
So yeah, we have the number of epochs

10:34:49.280 --> 10:34:50.720
and then we have the loss.

10:34:50.720 --> 10:34:53.120
So you could have a situation where you're having

10:34:53.120 --> 10:34:56.360
your training data, your training loss

10:34:56.360 --> 10:35:00.360
which keeps reducing whereas for the validation

10:35:00.360 --> 10:35:02.600
you see you would have something like this.

10:35:02.600 --> 10:35:07.600
So this is a typical plot for over feeding.

10:35:08.440 --> 10:35:11.340
Nonetheless in our case, the model over feeds

10:35:11.340 --> 10:35:12.720
but not that much.

10:35:12.720 --> 10:35:14.320
In some cases you will have a situation

10:35:14.320 --> 10:35:16.720
where this even starts to drop

10:35:16.720 --> 10:35:19.880
and where the loss starts to increase

10:35:19.880 --> 10:35:21.360
after a certain point.

10:35:21.360 --> 10:35:23.600
Here is the validation loss

10:35:23.600 --> 10:35:25.540
and then here is the training loss.

10:35:25.540 --> 10:35:29.280
Obviously the training loss will always keep reducing

10:35:29.280 --> 10:35:32.360
because we're training on this training data.

10:35:32.360 --> 10:35:35.200
So what we're saying is at this point

10:35:35.200 --> 10:35:38.740
where the validation stops reducing,

10:35:38.740 --> 10:35:41.680
it's important to just stop this training

10:35:41.680 --> 10:35:44.000
and this is known as early stopping.

10:35:44.000 --> 10:35:48.620
Now recall that the aim of callbacks

10:35:48.620 --> 10:35:52.280
is to be able to modify the training process,

10:35:52.280 --> 10:35:55.320
the evaluation process or the test process

10:35:55.320 --> 10:36:00.220
as a prediction process in an automatic manner.

10:36:00.220 --> 10:36:05.220
So that said, we shall make use of this early stopping

10:36:05.300 --> 10:36:08.280
callback right here which will permit us

10:36:08.280 --> 10:36:12.240
stop training automatically once we notice

10:36:12.240 --> 10:36:15.400
that a given parameter like say the loss,

10:36:15.400 --> 10:36:18.760
the validation loss doesn't drop any longer.

10:36:19.780 --> 10:36:21.960
So here we're just gonna copy this

10:36:21.960 --> 10:36:25.740
and then we just apply it similarly

10:36:25.740 --> 10:36:28.560
to while we are done with the CSV callback.

10:36:28.560 --> 10:36:32.120
So here we add this text and then we add that code,

10:36:32.120 --> 10:36:33.480
we just paste this out.

10:36:34.460 --> 10:36:36.200
Now we define this AS callback,

10:36:36.200 --> 10:36:38.200
that's early stopping callback

10:36:38.200 --> 10:36:39.960
and then we look at the significance

10:36:39.960 --> 10:36:42.600
of each and every one of these arguments.

10:36:42.600 --> 10:36:44.240
Now coming back to documentation,

10:36:44.240 --> 10:36:47.600
we have this monitor, quantity to be monitored.

10:36:47.600 --> 10:36:51.780
So by default, here we have this valid loss.

10:36:51.780 --> 10:36:55.920
This means that this callback will simply check

10:36:55.920 --> 10:36:59.340
on this validation loss right here

10:36:59.340 --> 10:37:04.340
and then once it stops reducing like see at this point,

10:37:04.340 --> 10:37:06.100
we're gonna stop the training.

10:37:07.560 --> 10:37:11.060
Whereas if we change this to say validation precision

10:37:11.060 --> 10:37:15.620
or validation accuracy, then what we'll be monitoring

10:37:15.620 --> 10:37:20.320
will be that accuracy or the precision value.

10:37:20.320 --> 10:37:21.960
So if you have them like this,

10:37:21.960 --> 10:37:24.440
we'll see we'll stop on this right here

10:37:24.440 --> 10:37:27.880
to ensure that we don't go and over feed

10:37:27.880 --> 10:37:29.120
on the training data.

10:37:29.960 --> 10:37:34.140
The next argument is this mean delta argument right here.

10:37:34.140 --> 10:37:36.280
So with the mean delta argument,

10:37:36.280 --> 10:37:38.720
we are defining a minimum change

10:37:38.720 --> 10:37:43.720
below which any change is considered as no improvement.

10:37:45.760 --> 10:37:48.080
So if we have a loss like this

10:37:49.360 --> 10:37:54.360
and that our mean delta is say a value of 0.1,

10:37:54.720 --> 10:37:59.720
then even if this loss reduces by a value of 0.5,

10:38:00.760 --> 10:38:03.080
then this callback will consider

10:38:03.080 --> 10:38:07.360
that there has been no decrease in the loss

10:38:07.360 --> 10:38:10.360
because the mean delta is 0.1.

10:38:10.360 --> 10:38:13.040
Now by default, the mean delta is set to zero.

10:38:13.040 --> 10:38:16.360
This means that any slight change is considered as a drop.

10:38:16.360 --> 10:38:19.720
So if we have even 0.0005,

10:38:19.720 --> 10:38:23.920
then we consider this as a drop in the loss.

10:38:25.360 --> 10:38:28.920
Now this is important because this has a patience,

10:38:28.920 --> 10:38:31.720
this callback makes use of this patience.

10:38:31.720 --> 10:38:35.880
With this patience, we are defining the number of epochs

10:38:35.880 --> 10:38:40.880
above which if we don't have a decrease

10:38:41.560 --> 10:38:43.240
in the validation loss,

10:38:43.240 --> 10:38:45.280
like in this case of the validation loss,

10:38:45.280 --> 10:38:50.240
we consider that we could stop that training process.

10:38:50.240 --> 10:38:55.240
And for the accuracy is the number of epochs above which

10:38:55.560 --> 10:38:59.320
if we don't have an increase in the validation accuracy,

10:38:59.320 --> 10:39:03.080
if we've picked validation accuracy for the monitor,

10:39:03.080 --> 10:39:05.640
then we'll have to stop the training.

10:39:05.640 --> 10:39:08.960
So we define this, well, we predefined this

10:39:08.960 --> 10:39:12.600
so that this could run automatically.

10:39:12.600 --> 10:39:16.000
Now for the mode, by default, we have the auto mode,

10:39:16.000 --> 10:39:18.880
but we could specify mean or max.

10:39:18.880 --> 10:39:21.360
Notice that when speaking about the loss,

10:39:21.360 --> 10:39:26.360
we spoke of a value or the number of epochs

10:39:27.640 --> 10:39:31.160
above which if the loss doesn't decrease.

10:39:31.160 --> 10:39:32.680
So here we're supposing that the loss

10:39:32.680 --> 10:39:34.600
is meant to be decreasing.

10:39:34.600 --> 10:39:38.000
And in that case, we are having a mode of mean.

10:39:38.000 --> 10:39:40.400
Now for the accuracy, we spoke of,

10:39:40.400 --> 10:39:43.960
for the patience, number of epochs above which

10:39:43.960 --> 10:39:46.680
if the accuracy doesn't increase.

10:39:46.680 --> 10:39:49.380
So in this case, we're having the max.

10:39:49.380 --> 10:39:53.000
Now, what TensorFlow permits us to do is to use an auto.

10:39:53.000 --> 10:39:56.760
And with this auto, TensorFlow automatically infers

10:39:56.760 --> 10:39:59.940
whether it's dealing with a mean or max.

10:39:59.940 --> 10:40:02.640
So this means that if you place in, for example,

10:40:02.640 --> 10:40:06.080
a valve position, this auto should be able to understand

10:40:06.080 --> 10:40:09.040
that a position should be increasing.

10:40:09.040 --> 10:40:10.680
And so it's going to use a max.

10:40:11.880 --> 10:40:15.180
Then we move to the baseline where the training stops

10:40:15.180 --> 10:40:19.880
if the model doesn't show improvement over the baseline.

10:40:19.880 --> 10:40:22.960
And finally, we have this restore best weights.

10:40:22.960 --> 10:40:27.840
With the restore best weights, which by default is false,

10:40:27.840 --> 10:40:31.200
we are simply saying that the model is going to take up

10:40:31.200 --> 10:40:32.840
its final state.

10:40:32.840 --> 10:40:37.720
So this means that if we start monitoring the model,

10:40:37.720 --> 10:40:40.680
say at this point here, or at this point where we have

10:40:40.680 --> 10:40:45.200
the lowest possible valve loss, and then the model,

10:40:45.200 --> 10:40:47.220
let's say we have a patience of five.

10:40:47.220 --> 10:40:50.680
So we are going to train for four to five epochs

10:40:50.680 --> 10:40:52.000
before stopping.

10:40:52.000 --> 10:40:54.320
So if after five epochs, we are on this,

10:40:54.320 --> 10:40:57.000
let's suppose that the feed epoch, we are on this.

10:40:57.000 --> 10:41:00.840
So we've added from here plus five epochs, we are on this.

10:41:00.840 --> 10:41:03.120
And then we are having this loss.

10:41:03.120 --> 10:41:06.020
It's clear that this model with this loss

10:41:06.020 --> 10:41:08.880
is less performant than this one.

10:41:08.880 --> 10:41:12.960
Now, if this restore best weights is set to false,

10:41:12.960 --> 10:41:16.600
then we'll just take the model's weights here,

10:41:16.600 --> 10:41:20.280
or the model weights which give this loss value here.

10:41:20.280 --> 10:41:23.520
Whereas if it's set to true, then it means we're going to

10:41:23.520 --> 10:41:27.240
take the best weights we've had throughout the training

10:41:27.240 --> 10:41:30.600
process and which happens to be the weights

10:41:30.600 --> 10:41:34.000
which provide this loss right here.

10:41:34.000 --> 10:41:35.560
That said, here we go.

10:41:35.560 --> 10:41:39.640
We have this, we have, let's say the patience to three,

10:41:39.640 --> 10:41:44.640
verbosity one, mode auto, baseline known,

10:41:45.080 --> 10:41:46.700
restore best weights false.

10:41:46.700 --> 10:41:50.200
So let's run that, and then all we need to do

10:41:50.200 --> 10:41:52.760
is to just include this year.

10:41:52.760 --> 10:41:56.420
So here we're going to have ES callback.

10:41:56.420 --> 10:41:58.480
Now we could take off the CSV callback,

10:41:58.480 --> 10:42:00.920
but you could always put all this together.

10:42:00.920 --> 10:42:04.280
Let's just let it, so we could see how all that works.

10:42:04.280 --> 10:42:06.240
So we just have this list right here,

10:42:06.240 --> 10:42:10.040
and we have this ES callback with the CSV callback.

10:42:10.040 --> 10:42:11.480
Now we run the training.

10:42:12.480 --> 10:42:14.600
We didn't train this for long enough to be able

10:42:14.600 --> 10:42:19.360
to observe any callback changes.

10:42:19.360 --> 10:42:21.960
So let's take this to 10 epochs,

10:42:21.960 --> 10:42:26.840
and then we reduce this to one or two.

10:42:26.840 --> 10:42:28.680
Let's take this to two.

10:42:28.680 --> 10:42:31.920
We run that again, so that's fine.

10:42:31.920 --> 10:42:35.040
And then we fit our model.

10:42:35.040 --> 10:42:37.360
After training for eight epochs,

10:42:37.360 --> 10:42:41.300
we see clearly here how the early stop in callback

10:42:41.300 --> 10:42:43.480
stops the training process.

10:42:43.480 --> 10:42:46.520
Now let's understand why this is,

10:42:46.520 --> 10:42:48.880
all this training process has been stopped.

10:42:48.880 --> 10:42:53.040
If you take a look at this validation loss right here,

10:42:53.040 --> 10:42:58.040
you would find that there was a drop here, drop increase,

10:42:58.040 --> 10:42:59.840
but after this increase, there was a drop.

10:42:59.840 --> 10:43:03.760
And since the patience is equal to,

10:43:03.760 --> 10:43:07.280
that's the patience we had defined here equal to,

10:43:07.280 --> 10:43:11.280
we have to get two successive increases

10:43:11.280 --> 10:43:15.860
or two successive same loss values

10:43:15.860 --> 10:43:18.620
before the training process could be stopped.

10:43:18.620 --> 10:43:21.000
So since we, after this, we have a drop,

10:43:21.000 --> 10:43:22.620
the training process continues,

10:43:22.620 --> 10:43:24.080
then we have this increase,

10:43:24.080 --> 10:43:26.760
and then we have this drop, so it continues.

10:43:26.760 --> 10:43:28.280
Then here we have this increase,

10:43:28.280 --> 10:43:30.680
and then here again, we have this other increase.

10:43:30.680 --> 10:43:34.160
So because now we have had this two successive increases,

10:43:35.020 --> 10:43:37.480
as we could see in the plot right here,

10:43:37.480 --> 10:43:38.820
you see right here,

10:43:40.120 --> 10:43:43.240
where we have this increase, drop, increase,

10:43:43.240 --> 10:43:44.540
and then increase.

10:43:46.000 --> 10:43:48.520
We now have the training process,

10:43:48.520 --> 10:43:52.820
which has been stopped, as we could see here.

10:43:52.820 --> 10:43:56.200
That said, we now move on to the learning rate scheduling.

10:44:00.280 --> 10:44:04.240
Up to this point, we've been used to training our models

10:44:04.240 --> 10:44:08.440
with one fixed learning rate throughout the process.

10:44:08.440 --> 10:44:13.440
So we could fix our learning rate as we did to say 0.01,

10:44:13.560 --> 10:44:15.640
and we use this same learning rate

10:44:15.640 --> 10:44:18.560
throughout our whole training process.

10:44:18.560 --> 10:44:21.880
But it happens that if this learning rate is too large,

10:44:21.880 --> 10:44:25.200
then we reach diverging,

10:44:25.200 --> 10:44:28.380
and if the learning rate is too small,

10:44:29.400 --> 10:44:32.600
it would take too long for our model to converge.

10:44:32.600 --> 10:44:35.000
So let's consider this plot right here.

10:44:35.000 --> 10:44:36.760
It's actually a very simplified plot,

10:44:36.760 --> 10:44:39.720
as actually what really happens

10:44:39.720 --> 10:44:41.600
is way more complex than this.

10:44:41.600 --> 10:44:45.960
So let's consider this plot, and we have this,

10:44:47.240 --> 10:44:49.840
and here we have the loss.

10:44:49.840 --> 10:44:53.640
Recall our aim and the weights.

10:44:53.640 --> 10:44:57.240
So here we have loss and then weights or parameters.

10:44:57.240 --> 10:45:00.360
And our aim is actually to modify this weights

10:45:00.360 --> 10:45:03.080
such that the loss is minimized.

10:45:03.080 --> 10:45:07.120
So our aim is to get to this position right here.

10:45:08.500 --> 10:45:11.460
Now we start with a case where we have a high learning rate.

10:45:11.460 --> 10:45:13.580
So if we were dealing with a high learning rate,

10:45:13.580 --> 10:45:16.480
then it would be easier for our model

10:45:16.480 --> 10:45:20.680
to find its way to this minimum position right here,

10:45:20.680 --> 10:45:23.400
to some position close to this minimum position,

10:45:23.400 --> 10:45:25.360
let's say at this point here.

10:45:26.200 --> 10:45:29.800
But the problem here is once it gets to this position,

10:45:29.800 --> 10:45:33.000
it could also very easily diverge from it.

10:45:33.000 --> 10:45:35.120
So it could very easily get back

10:45:35.120 --> 10:45:37.240
to another point around here,

10:45:37.240 --> 10:45:39.800
and then repeat this kind of process again

10:45:39.800 --> 10:45:42.940
where the model just kind of diverges.

10:45:42.940 --> 10:45:45.140
So you could have something like this,

10:45:45.140 --> 10:45:48.640
it could come down here and then get back

10:45:48.640 --> 10:45:51.620
to some point around this and so on and so forth.

10:45:51.620 --> 10:45:54.280
So we may have this case where the model

10:45:54.280 --> 10:45:56.440
doesn't really converge because the learning rate

10:45:56.440 --> 10:45:57.640
is too high.

10:45:57.640 --> 10:46:00.700
And then for the small learning rates,

10:46:02.160 --> 10:46:05.880
we may start training and then if say the model

10:46:05.880 --> 10:46:09.060
or if we find ourselves at this point here,

10:46:09.060 --> 10:46:11.000
that is we've modified the weights

10:46:11.000 --> 10:46:14.800
so that the loss value happens to be at this point here,

10:46:14.800 --> 10:46:19.800
it becomes difficult for us to get to this ultimate

10:46:21.720 --> 10:46:24.260
or global minima.

10:46:25.340 --> 10:46:27.360
Since the learning rate is too small

10:46:27.360 --> 10:46:30.560
and it changes, we're making very small changes.

10:46:30.560 --> 10:46:34.520
So we may find ourselves just staying around

10:46:34.520 --> 10:46:37.640
this local minima here instead of going

10:46:37.640 --> 10:46:40.520
towards this global minima, which is this.

10:46:41.760 --> 10:46:44.800
And so to bring in a balance, what we could do is

10:46:44.800 --> 10:46:47.400
when we start the training process,

10:46:47.400 --> 10:46:51.880
we could use a relatively high learning rate

10:46:51.880 --> 10:46:55.120
so that the model kind of approaches

10:46:55.120 --> 10:46:57.200
this global minima faster.

10:46:57.200 --> 10:47:00.340
And then after a certain number of epochs,

10:47:00.340 --> 10:47:04.000
we start or we modify the learning rate

10:47:04.000 --> 10:47:07.800
so that it becomes or it takes in very small values.

10:47:07.800 --> 10:47:09.600
And since it now takes in small values,

10:47:09.600 --> 10:47:13.120
we now start taking up very small changes

10:47:13.120 --> 10:47:17.600
such that we can get towards this global minima now

10:47:17.600 --> 10:47:19.720
without risking divergence.

10:47:21.160 --> 10:47:25.560
Now, one way of doing this is by say you could fix,

10:47:25.560 --> 10:47:28.480
let's say you could suppose that for the first 10 epochs,

10:47:28.480 --> 10:47:33.060
you train your model at a learning rate of say 0.1.

10:47:33.060 --> 10:47:36.600
And then from 10 to 20, you're gonna train the model

10:47:36.600 --> 10:47:39.080
a learner of say 0.01.

10:47:39.080 --> 10:47:41.120
Let's say we're dividing by a factor of 10.

10:47:41.120 --> 10:47:45.400
So we now move to 0.01 and the next 10 again,

10:47:45.400 --> 10:47:47.480
let's say we're training all this for 30 epochs

10:47:47.480 --> 10:47:51.360
and then the next we go to 0.001.

10:47:51.360 --> 10:47:54.400
So you could train your model and then after 10 epochs,

10:47:54.400 --> 10:47:57.480
you restart the training by modifying the learning rate

10:47:57.480 --> 10:48:01.300
in your optimizer and then again, you do this same year.

10:48:01.300 --> 10:48:05.360
But now the problem with this is you always have to be there

10:48:05.360 --> 10:48:07.720
to ensure that after the training,

10:48:07.720 --> 10:48:11.720
as after the 10 epochs, you modify this manually.

10:48:11.720 --> 10:48:14.160
Now, what if we're able to do this automatically?

10:48:15.040 --> 10:48:18.980
As usual, this is made possible by TensorFlow callbacks.

10:48:18.980 --> 10:48:21.220
With this callback, that's the learning rate

10:48:21.220 --> 10:48:24.720
shadow log callback, we could define a function

10:48:24.720 --> 10:48:27.000
which takes in the number of epochs

10:48:27.000 --> 10:48:29.960
and then modifies the learning rate

10:48:29.960 --> 10:48:32.600
based on the current epoch,

10:48:34.560 --> 10:48:37.720
based on a mixture of the current epoch

10:48:37.720 --> 10:48:40.500
and some predefined function.

10:48:41.560 --> 10:48:44.240
So as you could see here, we have the learning rate

10:48:44.240 --> 10:48:47.360
shadow log, it takes in a meta shadow

10:48:47.360 --> 10:48:50.480
and then we could specify the verbosity.

10:48:50.480 --> 10:48:54.520
Now, this is an example of this shadow log method

10:48:54.520 --> 10:48:56.040
which has been defined here.

10:48:56.040 --> 10:48:59.840
Here what it do is if the number of epochs is less than 10,

10:48:59.840 --> 10:49:02.640
then you're gonna use this predefined learning rate.

10:49:02.640 --> 10:49:06.160
And then in the case where the number of epochs

10:49:06.160 --> 10:49:08.040
is greater than or equal 10,

10:49:08.040 --> 10:49:12.400
then we start to reduce this learning rate

10:49:12.400 --> 10:49:14.100
in an exponential manner.

10:49:14.100 --> 10:49:17.520
So there we go, let's take this off.

10:49:17.520 --> 10:49:20.600
Now, what we're saying here is we're modifying

10:49:20.600 --> 10:49:23.520
the learning rate such that after that,

10:49:23.520 --> 10:49:25.360
we have in before 10 epochs,

10:49:25.360 --> 10:49:27.840
we have in this fixed learning rate.

10:49:27.840 --> 10:49:29.920
That's it.

10:49:29.920 --> 10:49:33.440
And then after this, we start to reduce this.

10:49:33.440 --> 10:49:35.120
So the learning rate starts dropping

10:49:35.120 --> 10:49:37.960
as we continue with the training.

10:49:37.960 --> 10:49:41.480
So now we don't really need to be monitoring

10:49:41.480 --> 10:49:44.580
the training manually because this callback

10:49:44.580 --> 10:49:48.680
will automatically modify the learning rate for you.

10:49:48.680 --> 10:49:50.480
We could simply copy out this example

10:49:50.480 --> 10:49:52.480
which has been given to us right here

10:49:52.480 --> 10:49:56.400
and then make use of it in our training process.

10:49:56.400 --> 10:50:00.120
So here we have to include this text

