10:52:54.200 --> 10:52:57.600
So we're expecting that below three epochs,

10:52:57.600 --> 10:52:59.480
let's say equal three epochs.

10:52:59.480 --> 10:53:01.780
We have a given learning rate and then above that

10:53:01.780 --> 10:53:05.680
we have a learning rate which decreases exponentially.

10:53:05.680 --> 10:53:06.680
So that's it.

10:53:08.960 --> 10:53:11.160
You see here that as the training process starts,

10:53:11.160 --> 10:53:13.260
we did not really need to print out any value

10:53:13.260 --> 10:53:18.160
because when we set our verbose to one, it outputs this.

10:53:18.160 --> 10:53:19.560
So here we have learning rate,

10:53:19.560 --> 10:53:23.240
scheduler setting the learning rate to 0.00999.

10:53:23.240 --> 10:53:25.680
That's practically 0.01,

10:53:25.680 --> 10:53:29.320
which is what we had given here in this learning rate.

10:53:29.320 --> 10:53:31.520
So it's practically this learning rate setting.

10:53:31.520 --> 10:53:34.900
And then as time goes on, it's going to decrease its value

10:53:34.900 --> 10:53:38.340
and then always output the current learning rate.

10:53:39.480 --> 10:53:42.160
So as we carry on the straining process,

10:53:42.160 --> 10:53:46.220
recall that the aim of having to work

10:53:46.220 --> 10:53:48.920
with these kinds of learning rate schedulers

10:53:48.920 --> 10:53:53.920
is that we want to actually get the best of both worlds.

10:53:54.160 --> 10:53:58.360
So what we want is speed because a very slow

10:53:58.360 --> 10:54:01.600
or a very small learning rate doesn't actually speed.

10:54:01.600 --> 10:54:03.860
And so we want speed.

10:54:03.860 --> 10:54:08.760
And then we also want stability when training.

10:54:08.760 --> 10:54:10.440
So because we want this tool,

10:54:10.440 --> 10:54:14.400
we are going to use or modify our learning rates

10:54:14.400 --> 10:54:19.000
such that we always get this through our training process.

10:54:19.000 --> 10:54:22.040
And so that's why whenever we started our training,

10:54:22.040 --> 10:54:25.920
we have high learning rates, which could ensure speed.

10:54:25.920 --> 10:54:28.600
And then as soon as we have trained

10:54:28.600 --> 10:54:32.360
for a given period of time or for a given number of epochs

10:54:32.360 --> 10:54:33.720
and that we're trying to approach

10:54:33.720 --> 10:54:36.200
this global minima right here,

10:54:36.200 --> 10:54:40.440
we now seek for stability by reducing the learning rate

10:54:40.440 --> 10:54:43.940
such that we don't get to this point and have to diverge.

10:54:43.940 --> 10:54:48.400
So if you want a more stable kind of training process,

10:54:48.400 --> 10:54:51.620
what we'll do is reduce this learning rate.

10:54:52.840 --> 10:54:55.340
After the training is complete, here's what we get.

10:54:55.340 --> 10:54:58.060
You could see that we have this learning rate,

10:54:58.060 --> 10:55:02.600
which is now modified after a given number of epochs.

10:55:02.600 --> 10:55:06.600
So here you see how the learning rate starts decreasing

10:55:06.600 --> 10:55:10.300
as we go on with the training process.

10:55:11.360 --> 10:55:14.700
And so that's how we implement learning rate scheduling.

10:55:14.700 --> 10:55:19.080
But before moving on, let's check out this tutorial

10:55:19.080 --> 10:55:23.680
provided by the MXNet project developers,

10:55:23.680 --> 10:55:26.880
which talks of other different learning rate

10:55:26.880 --> 10:55:28.680
scheduling techniques.

10:55:28.680 --> 10:55:32.520
So here we have this learning rate scheduling with warmup

10:55:32.520 --> 10:55:35.520
as a slanted triangular.

10:55:35.520 --> 10:55:39.400
And as you could see, the learning rate

10:55:39.400 --> 10:55:42.480
actually falls between these two values here.

10:55:42.480 --> 10:55:47.480
So we have a learning rate between one and two.

10:55:47.680 --> 10:55:51.440
So you could always define your max learning rate

10:55:51.440 --> 10:55:53.320
and then your mean learning rate.

10:55:53.320 --> 10:55:55.000
So you could always have this.

10:55:55.000 --> 10:55:59.240
And then with the warmup, what we'll do is

10:55:59.240 --> 10:56:04.240
we are going to increase this learning rate linearly

10:56:04.760 --> 10:56:09.760
up to the max for a given number of epochs.

10:56:10.140 --> 10:56:14.980
And then once we get to this number of epochs,

10:56:14.980 --> 10:56:18.860
we now start decreasing this learning rate.

10:56:18.860 --> 10:56:21.200
So here the decrease is linear.

10:56:21.200 --> 10:56:23.240
So it's a linear function we're using.

10:56:23.240 --> 10:56:25.720
And then once it gets to the minimum learning rate,

10:56:25.720 --> 10:56:29.280
we just maintain that constant value right up to the end.

10:56:29.280 --> 10:56:34.280
So another learning rate scheduling technique

10:56:34.840 --> 10:56:38.100
we could use here is we have this linear increase.

10:56:38.100 --> 10:56:39.960
That's the warmup.

10:56:39.960 --> 10:56:44.040
And then we decrease this exponentially

10:56:44.040 --> 10:56:46.200
right up to this minimum value.

10:56:47.400 --> 10:56:51.340
Now this technique of warmup was developed in this paper

10:56:51.340 --> 10:56:54.480
by Priya Goyal et al.

10:56:54.480 --> 10:56:57.880
And they found that having a smooth linear warmup

10:56:57.880 --> 10:57:00.400
in the learning rate at the start of the training

10:57:00.400 --> 10:57:02.700
improved the stability of the optimizer

10:57:02.700 --> 10:57:05.260
and led to better solutions.

10:57:05.260 --> 10:57:07.440
Then from here, we move on to the cosine

10:57:07.440 --> 10:57:09.520
aligning scheduling method,

10:57:09.520 --> 10:57:14.240
where there is this smooth decrease in the linear rate,

10:57:14.240 --> 10:57:18.340
which is kind of resembling the cosine function.

10:57:18.340 --> 10:57:21.800
Now this is what a cosine function actually looks like.

10:57:21.800 --> 10:57:25.600
So here we have this and that.

10:57:25.600 --> 10:57:28.260
So here is our cosine function.

10:57:28.260 --> 10:57:32.600
And then if you notice, you'll find that this portion

10:57:32.600 --> 10:57:36.440
actually looks similar to what we have here.

10:57:36.440 --> 10:57:39.240
And now after we get to a given number of epochs,

10:57:39.240 --> 10:57:41.000
we just maintain that.

10:57:41.000 --> 10:57:43.720
So we have defined the mean and then the max.

10:57:43.720 --> 10:57:44.760
And then once we get to the mean,

10:57:44.760 --> 10:57:47.200
we just maintain that mean throughout.

10:57:47.200 --> 10:57:51.240
Then we also have this stepwise decay scheduling.

10:57:51.240 --> 10:57:52.480
Let's take this off.

10:57:52.480 --> 10:57:55.480
Here you see how we start with a warmup.

10:57:55.480 --> 10:57:59.280
So this warmup is kind of like a method

10:57:59.280 --> 10:58:01.680
used very much in practice.

10:58:01.680 --> 10:58:05.900
And then from here we have this stepwise reductions.

10:58:05.900 --> 10:58:09.560
So we go in for this fixed learning rate

10:58:09.560 --> 10:58:11.480
after a given number of epochs,

10:58:11.480 --> 10:58:13.640
we now drop the learning rate

10:58:13.640 --> 10:58:15.700
and then we drop it and so on and so forth.

10:58:15.700 --> 10:58:20.700
So here's our stepwise scheduling with warmup.

10:58:21.280 --> 10:58:24.040
Then from here, we have this cool down

10:58:26.000 --> 10:58:28.360
where we follow the stepwise method.

10:58:28.360 --> 10:58:30.600
And then after a given number of epochs,

10:58:30.600 --> 10:58:33.960
we now reduce the learning rate linearly

10:58:33.960 --> 10:58:36.600
and the term cool down.

10:58:36.600 --> 10:58:41.600
We also have this one cycle scheduling technique

10:58:41.600 --> 10:58:45.740
proposed by Leslie and Smith and Nicolet Topin.

10:58:46.600 --> 10:58:48.180
And here's what it looks like.

10:58:48.180 --> 10:58:50.840
You see that we increase that we have this warmup

10:58:50.840 --> 10:58:53.240
and then this linear decrease.

10:58:53.240 --> 10:58:55.740
Once we get to this initial position,

10:58:55.740 --> 10:58:58.000
we also have again this linear decrease

10:58:58.000 --> 10:59:03.000
with a different slope, with a smaller slope.

10:59:03.400 --> 10:59:06.020
And then once we get to this minimum

10:59:06.020 --> 10:59:09.180
or this final minimum,

10:59:09.180 --> 10:59:12.600
we now just actually just maintain the learning rate.

10:59:13.880 --> 10:59:17.400
Now finally, we have the cyclical scheduling methods

10:59:18.520 --> 10:59:21.240
originally proposed by Leslie and Smith.

10:59:21.240 --> 10:59:23.680
The idea of cyclical increasing

10:59:23.680 --> 10:59:26.440
or cyclically increasing and decreasing

10:59:26.440 --> 10:59:27.860
the learning rate has been shown

10:59:27.860 --> 10:59:31.680
to give faster convergence and more optimal solutions.

10:59:31.680 --> 10:59:35.360
So as you can see here, we are having the learning rate

10:59:35.360 --> 10:59:37.140
which is going to be bouncing

10:59:37.140 --> 10:59:40.320
from the minimum to the maximum, as you can see here.

10:59:40.320 --> 10:59:43.240
So we have this linear increase to the max

10:59:43.240 --> 10:59:46.400
and then linear drop, linear increase and your drop

10:59:46.400 --> 10:59:48.400
and so on and so forth.

10:59:48.400 --> 10:59:53.400
And then finally, we have this cyclical cosine aniline

10:59:53.480 --> 10:59:54.760
scheduling right here.

10:59:54.760 --> 10:59:57.080
We see how we have the cosine aniline

10:59:57.080 --> 11:00:00.180
and then we have this full cyclical process

11:00:00.180 --> 11:00:03.040
as we go from the highest to the lowest.

11:00:03.040 --> 11:00:05.640
And then this next cycle from the meat

11:00:05.640 --> 11:00:08.080
between the mean and the max,

11:00:08.080 --> 11:00:12.680
that's this right to the mean learning rate.

11:00:12.680 --> 11:00:15.200
And then we take up this final cycle

11:00:15.200 --> 11:00:17.720
from this quarter of the total.

11:00:17.720 --> 11:00:20.960
So we had 0.5, which is a quarter of two.

11:00:20.960 --> 11:00:25.140
So we start from this and then right to the mean.

11:00:25.140 --> 11:00:29.420
Now notice also that as we go into the cycle,

11:00:29.420 --> 11:00:31.840
the cycle lengths keep increasing.

11:00:31.840 --> 11:00:36.840
So we move from this to this length and finally this length.

11:00:37.240 --> 11:00:39.480
Based on the scheduler you want to implement,

11:00:39.480 --> 11:00:41.600
all you need to do is to modify

11:00:41.600 --> 11:00:43.920
this scheduler method right here.

11:00:43.920 --> 11:00:45.920
The next callback we'll be looking at

11:00:45.920 --> 11:00:48.400
is that of model checkpointing.

11:00:48.400 --> 11:00:50.880
In this model checkpoint callback,

11:00:50.880 --> 11:00:55.760
we are able to save the model's weights at some frequency.

11:00:55.760 --> 11:00:59.880
So unlike previously where after training,

11:00:59.880 --> 11:01:02.600
like with this after training our model,

11:01:02.600 --> 11:01:07.600
we get to load or rather we get to save the model like here.

11:01:07.900 --> 11:01:12.080
So here we save the model, we save our weights.

11:01:12.080 --> 11:01:15.040
After we're done with the training here,

11:01:15.040 --> 11:01:19.040
we could be doing this weight saving

11:01:19.040 --> 11:01:21.000
during the training process.

11:01:21.000 --> 11:01:30.640
And thanks to this model checkpoint callback,

11:01:30.640 --> 11:01:34.440
now as usual, we just copy this and then we could check out

11:01:34.440 --> 11:01:38.320
on this documentation for the significance

11:01:38.320 --> 11:01:40.840
of each and every one of those arguments.

11:01:40.840 --> 11:01:42.440
Here we have the file path.

11:01:42.440 --> 11:01:49.720
So this is the file where we are going to be saving our model.

11:01:49.720 --> 11:01:54.840
So here we have our checkpoint callback and that's it.

11:01:54.840 --> 11:01:56.840
We have model checkpoint here.

11:01:56.840 --> 11:01:59.000
We're going to define this file path.

11:01:59.000 --> 11:02:00.800
And then what are we going to be monitoring?

11:02:00.800 --> 11:02:03.960
We're going to be monitoring the validation loss,

11:02:03.960 --> 11:02:07.760
which is what's given to us by default.

11:02:07.760 --> 11:02:10.160
Just like with the early stop-in,

11:02:10.160 --> 11:02:13.040
where we had this monitor right here,

11:02:13.040 --> 11:02:16.600
we are going to look at our validation loss.

11:02:16.600 --> 11:02:19.840
And supposing we have this validation loss

11:02:19.840 --> 11:02:21.600
and then our training loss.

11:02:21.600 --> 11:02:23.640
So we're going to look at our validation loss,

11:02:23.640 --> 11:02:28.120
which is this, and then save the model weights

11:02:28.120 --> 11:02:30.520
when we have this mini model

11:02:30.520 --> 11:02:33.920
or the smallest validation loss right here.

11:02:33.920 --> 11:02:38.280
And then if you set the save best only to true,

11:02:38.280 --> 11:02:40.960
then we'll save only this weights,

11:02:40.960 --> 11:02:43.920
whereas when it's false, we'll save this

11:02:43.920 --> 11:02:48.080
and also save the latest weights.

11:02:48.080 --> 11:02:51.800
And then for this argument save weights only,

11:02:51.800 --> 11:02:56.520
if it's set to true, then we're going to save only the weights,

11:02:56.520 --> 11:02:57.880
whereas if it's set to false,

11:02:57.880 --> 11:03:00.040
then we're going to be saving a model.

11:03:00.040 --> 11:03:04.320
That's a full model with its parameters.

11:03:04.320 --> 11:03:06.880
Then we have the mode, which is automatic,

11:03:06.880 --> 11:03:09.040
like since it's validation loss,

11:03:09.040 --> 11:03:12.440
since it's a loss automatically, this would be a mean.

11:03:12.440 --> 11:03:15.960
Whereas if this is an accuracy or precision,

11:03:15.960 --> 11:03:18.560
we're going to have a max right here.

11:03:18.560 --> 11:03:20.960
So let's take this back to auto

11:03:20.960 --> 11:03:24.400
and then we have validation loss.

11:03:24.400 --> 11:03:27.840
Now the save frequency when set to epoch simply means

11:03:27.840 --> 11:03:31.600
we're going to do the saving after each and every epoch,

11:03:31.600 --> 11:03:33.600
but we could modify this to say three.

11:03:33.600 --> 11:03:35.120
So after three epochs,

11:03:35.120 --> 11:03:38.440
we're going to verify if our current model

11:03:38.440 --> 11:03:41.880
is the best of all the previous models,

11:03:41.880 --> 11:03:44.240
that's if we've set this to true.

11:03:44.240 --> 11:03:46.000
So if we've set this to true,

11:03:46.000 --> 11:03:48.240
we're going to verify if this model,

11:03:48.240 --> 11:03:50.520
the current model is the best, if it's the best,

11:03:50.520 --> 11:03:53.720
then we override this file path,

11:03:53.720 --> 11:03:56.560
that's override the file, the weight file we've saved.

11:03:56.560 --> 11:03:59.600
And if it's not the best, we continue training.

11:03:59.600 --> 11:04:02.640
So that said, let's modify our file path

11:04:02.640 --> 11:04:06.480
and then let's call this check points.

11:04:06.480 --> 11:04:07.320
That's it.

11:04:07.320 --> 11:04:08.960
So we have the checkpoints

11:04:08.960 --> 11:04:11.840
and then we run this.

11:04:11.840 --> 11:04:15.040
So we have that.

11:04:15.040 --> 11:04:17.800
And then we add this callback right here.

11:04:17.800 --> 11:04:20.440
So we have the checkpoint,

11:04:20.440 --> 11:04:23.040
checkpoint callback.

11:04:23.040 --> 11:04:23.920
That's fine.

11:04:23.920 --> 11:04:26.420
We run the training now and see what we get.

11:04:28.000 --> 11:04:30.200
After training for over 10 epochs,

11:04:30.200 --> 11:04:33.200
we have this two logs, which you could notice here,

11:04:33.200 --> 11:04:35.880
this one where we've been told assets

11:04:35.880 --> 11:04:39.040
reaching to checkpoints assets.

11:04:39.040 --> 11:04:40.960
So you could open up this checkpoints here.

11:04:40.960 --> 11:04:44.880
And then you'll see that we actually saving this model.

11:04:44.880 --> 11:04:47.600
And then we also have this log here,

11:04:47.600 --> 11:04:49.840
that's after the seed epoch.

11:04:49.840 --> 11:04:52.760
Now let's understand why this login is done

11:04:52.760 --> 11:04:55.720
after the first and also after the seed epoch.

11:04:57.200 --> 11:04:58.600
Just when training starts,

11:04:58.600 --> 11:05:01.240
we have a validation loss of 0.47.

11:05:01.240 --> 11:05:04.720
And so we're supposing that since this is the first then,

11:05:04.720 --> 11:05:07.040
the model sets its best state.

11:05:07.040 --> 11:05:09.200
Then it moves to one, which is greater than this.

11:05:09.200 --> 11:05:12.440
So the model state or the best state

11:05:12.440 --> 11:05:15.000
is this one after the first epoch.

11:05:15.000 --> 11:05:17.400
This, the model, the first epoch

11:05:17.400 --> 11:05:20.480
or the model state of the first epoch is to the best.

11:05:20.480 --> 11:05:22.040
This doesn't beat the record.

11:05:22.040 --> 11:05:23.600
This doesn't.

11:05:23.600 --> 11:05:28.160
And then you'd see that here we have this validation loss

11:05:28.160 --> 11:05:30.520
of 0.24.

11:05:30.520 --> 11:05:33.640
That is why the model is now saved.

11:05:33.640 --> 11:05:36.280
From this model checkpoint and section,

11:05:36.280 --> 11:05:40.800
we'll look at the reduced learning rate on plateau.

11:05:40.800 --> 11:05:44.240
And then later on, we'll have a dedicated section

11:05:44.240 --> 11:05:45.720
for tensor board.

11:05:46.800 --> 11:05:50.000
The way this learning rate or rather reduced learning rate

11:05:50.000 --> 11:05:52.080
on plateau works is that,

11:05:52.080 --> 11:05:55.800
if you start training with a fixed learning rate,

11:05:55.800 --> 11:06:00.720
and then say you've trained for over 100 epochs,

11:06:00.720 --> 11:06:05.720
and that the model performance after 110 epochs,

11:06:05.960 --> 11:06:09.280
so let's say we have 110 right here.

11:06:09.280 --> 11:06:11.560
So after this 110 epochs,

11:06:11.560 --> 11:06:14.280
our model performance doesn't improve.

11:06:14.280 --> 11:06:17.200
So we still fix our learning rate.

11:06:17.200 --> 11:06:21.200
But since for the past 10 epochs,

11:06:21.200 --> 11:06:23.960
we haven't had an improvement in the model's performance,

11:06:23.960 --> 11:06:28.440
what we'll do is we'll reduce the model's learning rate

11:06:28.440 --> 11:06:30.200
by a given factor.

11:06:30.200 --> 11:06:32.880
So here is the factor.

11:06:32.880 --> 11:06:35.280
The patience, our patience here is 10 million

11:06:35.280 --> 11:06:37.080
that we're gonna wait for 10 epochs

11:06:37.080 --> 11:06:39.080
before deciding whether we're gonna reduce

11:06:39.080 --> 11:06:40.480
the learning rate or not.

11:06:40.480 --> 11:06:42.320
The verbosity as usual,

11:06:42.320 --> 11:06:45.280
the quantity to be monitored,

11:06:45.280 --> 11:06:48.880
in this case, the default is the validation loss,

11:06:48.880 --> 11:06:50.560
the mode automatic.

11:06:50.560 --> 11:06:52.880
So if you want it to be mean,

11:06:52.880 --> 11:06:56.520
you could specify mean or max.

11:06:56.520 --> 11:06:58.840
But setting it to automatic allows TensorFlow

11:06:58.840 --> 11:07:01.920
to automatically decide whether it's a mean or max.

11:07:01.920 --> 11:07:04.320
In the case of validation loss,

11:07:04.320 --> 11:07:05.760
obviously it's gonna be a mean

11:07:05.760 --> 11:07:08.280
since our aim is to reduce the validation loss

11:07:08.280 --> 11:07:09.640
as much as we can.

11:07:09.640 --> 11:07:12.120
Then we have the mean delta,

11:07:12.120 --> 11:07:16.560
which takes in a default value of 0.0001.

11:07:16.560 --> 11:07:21.360
And this means that if after say 10 epochs,

11:07:21.360 --> 11:07:24.680
we have a change in the validation loss,

11:07:24.680 --> 11:07:28.640
but this change is less than this mean delta,

11:07:28.640 --> 11:07:31.920
then we'll consider that that isn't a change.

11:07:31.920 --> 11:07:35.120
And hence we are gonna still reduce the learning rate

11:07:35.120 --> 11:07:37.120
by this factor right here.

11:07:37.120 --> 11:07:38.760
We also have the cool down

11:07:38.760 --> 11:07:41.360
and then the minimum learning rate

11:07:41.360 --> 11:07:45.880
below which we wouldn't wanna drop our learning rate.

11:07:45.880 --> 11:07:46.840
As described here,

11:07:46.840 --> 11:07:49.360
this cool down is a number of epochs to wait

11:07:49.360 --> 11:07:52.160
before you resuming normal operation

11:07:52.160 --> 11:07:54.920
after the learning rate has been reduced.

11:07:54.920 --> 11:07:58.040
We are gonna create this callback very easily.

11:07:58.040 --> 11:07:59.240
And this time around,

11:07:59.240 --> 11:08:01.680
we're gonna monitor the validation accuracy.

11:08:01.680 --> 11:08:05.360
So if the validation accuracy doesn't increase

11:08:05.360 --> 11:08:07.480
after two epochs,

11:08:07.480 --> 11:08:11.800
we are gonna reduce the learning rate by a factor of 0.1.

11:08:11.800 --> 11:08:15.800
Meaning that if we had a learning rate of say 0.01

11:08:18.080 --> 11:08:21.160
and that this validation accuracy

11:08:21.160 --> 11:08:24.520
hasn't increased after the two epochs,

11:08:24.520 --> 11:08:27.000
we are gonna reduce this such that

11:08:27.000 --> 11:08:32.000
this becomes 0.01 times 0.1,

11:08:32.800 --> 11:08:36.040
which is this factor right here.

11:08:36.040 --> 11:08:36.880
Just as before,

11:08:36.880 --> 11:08:39.680
we are gonna add this plateau callback

11:08:39.680 --> 11:08:42.560
and then train our model.

11:08:42.560 --> 11:08:44.760
After training for five epochs,

11:08:44.760 --> 11:08:48.800
we can observe that we have this learning rate

11:08:48.800 --> 11:08:52.800
which has been reduced after the third epoch.

11:08:52.800 --> 11:08:57.600
And this is because after these two epochs,

11:08:57.600 --> 11:09:02.600
there is no improvement in this initial validation accuracy.

11:09:02.680 --> 11:09:05.840
And so that's why the accuracy is gonna drop

11:09:05.840 --> 11:09:10.640
from 0.01 to 0.001.

11:09:10.640 --> 11:09:12.360
Now that's it for this section.

11:09:12.360 --> 11:09:14.100
Thank you for getting up to this point

11:09:14.100 --> 11:09:28.880
and see you next time.

11:09:28.880 --> 11:09:32.280
Hello everyone and welcome to this new section

11:09:32.280 --> 11:09:34.640
in which we're gonna look at different strategies

11:09:34.640 --> 11:09:37.560
of combating overfitting and underfitting.

11:09:37.560 --> 11:09:41.000
This strategies will include data augmentation

11:09:41.000 --> 11:09:44.360
as you could see here, dropout, regularization,

11:09:44.360 --> 11:09:47.280
early stopping, smaller network usage,

11:09:47.280 --> 11:09:50.700
hyperparameter tuning and normalization.

11:09:50.700 --> 11:09:51.960
So far in this class,

11:09:51.960 --> 11:09:56.280
we've mentioned the terms overfitting and underfitting

11:09:56.280 --> 11:09:58.180
without really getting in depth

11:09:58.180 --> 11:10:01.120
to what this two actually mean.

11:10:01.120 --> 11:10:04.840
Now for overfitting, we'll consider this plot

11:10:04.840 --> 11:10:08.120
of the loss versus the number of epochs

11:10:08.120 --> 11:10:11.320
and then precision versus number of epochs.

11:10:11.320 --> 11:10:13.600
With a loss versus number of epochs,

11:10:13.600 --> 11:10:15.760
what we'll generally have when a model

11:10:15.760 --> 11:10:17.840
overfitting something like this.

11:10:17.840 --> 11:10:22.560
So here we have the training, the validation loss

11:10:22.560 --> 11:10:25.980
and that here we have the training loss.

11:10:25.980 --> 11:10:30.980
So this is just like a general way of looking at this

11:10:31.160 --> 11:10:34.400
though this may take different forms.

11:10:34.400 --> 11:10:36.520
Now that said, as you could see,

11:10:36.520 --> 11:10:40.880
we have this two sets and the loss

11:10:40.880 --> 11:10:43.600
versus number of epochs plots.

11:10:43.600 --> 11:10:47.460
Clearly we could see that the validation

11:10:47.460 --> 11:10:50.520
and the training set initially start up

11:10:50.520 --> 11:10:52.800
with a similar pattern.

11:10:52.800 --> 11:10:55.120
Sometimes you may even have the validation

11:10:55.120 --> 11:10:56.280
which comes up like this.

11:10:56.280 --> 11:10:58.000
So you can even have a situation like this

11:10:58.000 --> 11:11:00.500
where the validation performs even better

11:11:00.500 --> 11:11:03.040
than the training set initially.

11:11:03.040 --> 11:11:06.600
But generally when a model overfeits,

11:11:06.600 --> 11:11:09.440
what we'll have will be this kind of model

11:11:09.440 --> 11:11:14.440
where at some point the model keeps doing well

11:11:15.160 --> 11:11:16.720
at the level of the training

11:11:16.720 --> 11:11:21.440
and starts doing very poorly in the validation set.

11:11:21.440 --> 11:11:25.440
We could replicate this with a precision epoch.

11:11:25.440 --> 11:11:27.940
This could be precision accuracy recall

11:11:27.940 --> 11:11:30.980
or some other metric which we've chosen.

11:11:30.980 --> 11:11:33.760
And what we could have will be something like this.

11:11:33.760 --> 11:11:35.720
Obviously you saw in the practice,

11:11:35.720 --> 11:11:40.200
it isn't always like this, but something in this sense.

11:11:40.200 --> 11:11:44.440
So you would have this here, you see you have the training

11:11:45.580 --> 11:11:48.760
and then you have the validation right here.

11:11:48.760 --> 11:11:52.920
So what goes on is your model keeps on doing very well

11:11:52.920 --> 11:11:55.860
for the training and then with a validation

11:11:55.860 --> 11:12:00.860
at some point it starts doing even a very, very poorly.

11:12:01.320 --> 11:12:04.360
And so the danger with this is if you are considering

11:12:04.360 --> 11:12:06.440
working only with a training set,

11:12:06.440 --> 11:12:09.960
you may feel like you're more epochs

11:12:09.960 --> 11:12:12.480
or training over more epoch is a good idea

11:12:12.480 --> 11:12:15.400
because you keep having this great results.

11:12:15.400 --> 11:12:18.720
Like supposing we have fixed this year

11:12:18.720 --> 11:12:21.920
and that your training is like say 99%.

11:12:21.920 --> 11:12:26.920
So you have 99% precision score on your training data

11:12:27.760 --> 11:12:30.880
and you're like, wow, this model will do very well

11:12:30.880 --> 11:12:32.960
in the real world.

11:12:32.960 --> 11:12:34.880
But this isn't generally the case

11:12:34.880 --> 11:12:39.880
because this model actually has been over-fitted

11:12:40.080 --> 11:12:41.780
on your training data.

11:12:41.780 --> 11:12:45.980
Your model has learned instead to modify its weights

11:12:45.980 --> 11:12:48.200
based on the training data

11:12:48.200 --> 11:12:53.200
instead of being able to extract useful information

11:12:54.640 --> 11:12:57.320
or some intelligence from the data

11:12:57.320 --> 11:13:00.440
which has been used in training this model.

11:13:00.440 --> 11:13:05.440
The main cause of overfitting is having a small data set

11:13:06.280 --> 11:13:10.320
and a large and complex model

11:13:10.320 --> 11:13:13.420
which contains so many parameters.

11:13:13.420 --> 11:13:17.800
Now, if a model like say deep neural network

11:13:17.800 --> 11:13:22.420
has many parameters and you're giving it a small data set,

11:13:22.420 --> 11:13:25.440
then obviously it's gonna adjust its parameters

11:13:25.440 --> 11:13:28.400
such that on this small data set,

11:13:28.400 --> 11:13:31.000
it performs exceptionally well.

11:13:31.000 --> 11:13:36.000
So you may come across a model which has say 99.9%

11:13:37.280 --> 11:13:39.360
precision or accuracy

11:13:39.360 --> 11:13:43.880
just because the data you've used to train the model

11:13:43.880 --> 11:13:47.120
has or was very small.

11:13:47.120 --> 11:13:51.120
Now, in another case, you may have say a moderate sized data

11:13:51.120 --> 11:13:55.440
set and then not just this kind of model

11:13:55.440 --> 11:13:57.900
but a very large model.

11:13:57.900 --> 11:14:01.920
So at the end of the day, we notice that there has to always

11:14:01.920 --> 11:14:06.300
be a balance between this data set and the model size.

11:14:06.300 --> 11:14:09.840
So this means that even if you increase the data size

11:14:09.840 --> 11:14:12.480
and then you also increase the model size,

11:14:12.480 --> 11:14:15.720
your model may still risk overfitting.

11:14:15.720 --> 11:14:18.240
To better understand this concept of overfitting,

11:14:18.240 --> 11:14:20.640
let's take this simple example.

11:14:20.640 --> 11:14:24.200
Supposing that you have three subjects to master a school,

11:14:24.200 --> 11:14:28.280
let's say math, English and sports.

11:14:28.280 --> 11:14:30.200
But when kids get to school,

11:14:30.200 --> 11:14:33.620
they are only taught mathematics

11:14:33.620 --> 11:14:36.560
or they only taught English or only taught sports.

11:14:36.560 --> 11:14:38.480
Let's choose for example, mathematics.

11:14:38.480 --> 11:14:42.540
So these kids get to school and from the first year

11:14:42.540 --> 11:14:46.040
to the last year, they taught only mathematics.

11:14:46.040 --> 11:14:49.520
It's clear that when you evaluate this kid

11:14:49.520 --> 11:14:52.780
or when you pick a kid at random and evaluate that kid

11:14:52.780 --> 11:14:56.800
in mathematics, the kid would tend to have a better

11:14:56.800 --> 11:15:01.420
or an above average result in mathematics

11:15:01.420 --> 11:15:03.740
compared to kids from other schools.

11:15:04.700 --> 11:15:08.180
But when tested on subjects like English and sports,

11:15:09.020 --> 11:15:11.040
that may not be the case.

11:15:11.040 --> 11:15:13.760
And this is because the English

11:15:13.760 --> 11:15:17.180
and sports weren't taught at school.

11:15:17.180 --> 11:15:20.960
And so if you are evaluated on only what you were taught,

11:15:20.960 --> 11:15:24.320
you would tend to have very high scores like this.

11:15:24.320 --> 11:15:26.480
And then what if we started to evaluate

11:15:26.480 --> 11:15:29.100
on stuff the kids were not taught?

11:15:29.100 --> 11:15:32.600
You'll notice that those kids will then start having

11:15:32.600 --> 11:15:36.920
poorer results because those kids haven't taken out

11:15:36.920 --> 11:15:40.360
some time to master English and sports.

11:15:40.360 --> 11:15:43.520
And so you may end up with a kid who was able to show

11:15:43.520 --> 11:15:48.200
for example, that sine squared x plus cos squared x

11:15:48.200 --> 11:15:53.200
equal one, but can't tell you the past tense of it.

11:15:54.360 --> 11:15:57.680
Now it's clear that to readjust this situation,

11:15:57.680 --> 11:16:01.400
the kids have to be taught all the subjects

11:16:01.400 --> 11:16:05.340
such that there is a balance that those kids need.

11:16:05.340 --> 11:16:09.240
And obviously this balance will come in a way

11:16:09.240 --> 11:16:13.320
that the kids now perform better in these two subjects.

11:16:13.320 --> 11:16:17.560
And because they have had part of the time

11:16:17.560 --> 11:16:21.140
they used to study maths allocated for English and sports,

11:16:21.140 --> 11:16:26.100
they may perform slightly less than before in maths,

11:16:26.100 --> 11:16:30.040
but at least what's important to notice this time around,

11:16:30.040 --> 11:16:33.720
they can now express themselves better in English

11:16:33.720 --> 11:16:35.440
or practice some sports.

11:16:35.440 --> 11:16:37.420
Let's now take this other example.

11:16:37.420 --> 11:16:41.120
Supposing we're training a model which predicts

11:16:41.120 --> 11:16:43.840
the presence of a car in an image,

11:16:43.840 --> 11:16:48.320
and then you feed your model with this kind of data.

11:16:48.320 --> 11:16:51.820
Then after training your model, where you get to test

11:16:51.820 --> 11:16:56.820
that model is on this kind of real world data.

11:16:57.140 --> 11:17:01.200
It's clear that even if you had very great results

11:17:01.200 --> 11:17:05.880
with a previous data, in this working with this or testing

11:17:05.880 --> 11:17:09.000
on this new data set right here,

11:17:09.000 --> 11:17:13.200
you wouldn't have those amazing results you had previously.

11:17:13.200 --> 11:17:16.940
And so it's important that not only should your data

11:17:16.940 --> 11:17:21.940
be large enough or get as much useful data as you can,

11:17:22.640 --> 11:17:26.760
but ensure that your training data represents

11:17:26.760 --> 11:17:30.680
or looks like your test data or looks like

11:17:30.680 --> 11:17:33.200
what you're going to be having in production,

11:17:33.200 --> 11:17:35.040
as that's really what matters.

11:17:35.040 --> 11:17:38.880
What matters is the fact that you have a model in production

11:17:38.880 --> 11:17:43.360
which works well, or which has a high accuracy

11:17:43.360 --> 11:17:46.320
and not a model in your notebook,

11:17:46.320 --> 11:17:50.780
which has say 100% accuracy on your training data,

11:17:50.780 --> 11:17:55.380
whereas on production, it doesn't perform very well.

11:17:56.480 --> 11:17:58.600
Now moving on to under feeding,

11:17:58.600 --> 11:18:03.600
it turns out that here our model becomes way too simple

11:18:03.600 --> 11:18:08.320
for it to even be able to extract information from our data.

11:18:08.320 --> 11:18:11.760
So we may have our validation data

11:18:11.760 --> 11:18:15.120
and then our training data like this.

11:18:15.120 --> 11:18:20.120
And then there is this huge gap between our current loss

11:18:21.000 --> 11:18:23.440
and the minimum possible loss.

11:18:23.440 --> 11:18:26.440
We could also have this at a level of say,

11:18:26.440 --> 11:18:27.880
let's say accuracy.

11:18:27.880 --> 11:18:30.000
So yeah, we could have accuracy

11:18:30.000 --> 11:18:32.820
and then we have our validation

11:18:32.820 --> 11:18:37.820
and then training and we have say accuracy 100%.

11:18:38.240 --> 11:18:39.840
Let's say we have one right here

11:18:39.840 --> 11:18:43.640
and then our model is still too simple

11:18:43.640 --> 11:18:48.640
that we just end up say at 0.6 or 60% accuracy.

11:18:50.600 --> 11:18:52.680
In this kind of situation,

11:18:52.680 --> 11:18:56.480
our data or the relative size of our data

11:18:57.720 --> 11:19:00.880
as compared to the model may be too large.

11:19:00.880 --> 11:19:02.580
So you could even have a situation

11:19:02.580 --> 11:19:07.020
where this data is smaller than what we had here.

11:19:07.020 --> 11:19:10.060
So your data could be small like this,

11:19:10.060 --> 11:19:14.080
but if your model is way too simple,

11:19:14.080 --> 11:19:17.040
say we have just this very small model,

11:19:17.040 --> 11:19:21.640
then you may face this problem of under feeding.

11:19:21.640 --> 11:19:24.720
It also turns out that sometimes you may have a situation

11:19:24.720 --> 11:19:28.640
where you have even a very complex model,

11:19:28.640 --> 11:19:31.040
but that model still under feeds.

11:19:31.040 --> 11:19:35.000
And that's because that model hasn't been built in a way

11:19:35.000 --> 11:19:40.000
that it could extract useful information from this data.

11:19:40.080 --> 11:19:42.240
Now, if you could recall in the section

11:19:42.240 --> 11:19:44.640
where we're predicting the car price,

11:19:44.640 --> 11:19:46.880
we had a situation where a model,

11:19:46.880 --> 11:19:49.440
we use a simple dense layer.

11:19:49.440 --> 11:19:52.800
We use a simple single dense layer

11:19:52.800 --> 11:19:57.800
with just two parameters and we have this fixed data set.

11:19:57.800 --> 11:20:02.800
But once we increased our stacked up more dense layers,

11:20:05.800 --> 11:20:09.660
we found out that we're able to get better training

11:20:09.660 --> 11:20:13.880
and validation mean average error values.

11:20:13.880 --> 11:20:16.360
There are several ways in which we could mitigate

11:20:16.360 --> 11:20:18.240
this problem of over feeding.

11:20:18.240 --> 11:20:19.840
The very first one we'll look at

11:20:19.840 --> 11:20:22.080
is that of collecting more data.

11:20:22.080 --> 11:20:27.080
So it's important to lay hands on as much data

11:20:27.080 --> 11:20:28.760
as you can.

11:20:28.760 --> 11:20:30.560
This data has to be representative

11:20:30.560 --> 11:20:33.360
of what the model would see in real life.

11:20:33.360 --> 11:20:36.140
And this data should be as diverse as possible.

11:20:37.320 --> 11:20:39.660
Even after collecting more data,

11:20:39.660 --> 11:20:41.440
to solve this problem of over feeding,

11:20:41.440 --> 11:20:44.560
we could use data augmentation.

11:20:44.560 --> 11:20:47.200
Now, what is data augmentation all about?

11:20:47.200 --> 11:20:50.240
Supposing we have this image cell right here,

11:20:50.240 --> 11:20:52.040
the cell image we have here,

11:20:53.120 --> 11:20:55.980
which happens to be pasteurized.

11:20:55.980 --> 11:21:00.320
Now, instead of having just this in our data set,

11:21:00.320 --> 11:21:04.360
we could have this image right here modified

11:21:04.360 --> 11:21:07.960
such that we now have more data to train on.

11:21:07.960 --> 11:21:10.780
So this means that in the case where initially

11:21:10.780 --> 11:21:15.240
we had say 20,000 images,

11:21:15.240 --> 11:21:17.200
so we have 20,000 images.

11:21:17.200 --> 11:21:19.360
Now, after doing data augmentation,

11:21:19.360 --> 11:21:21.400
after modifying each and every image,

11:21:21.400 --> 11:21:25.600
we now have a data set of 80,000.

11:21:25.600 --> 11:21:27.240
Now we're considering 80,000

11:21:27.240 --> 11:21:29.480
because we're supposing that each image

11:21:29.480 --> 11:21:32.860
is gonna be flipped as we've just done right here.

11:21:32.860 --> 11:21:34.680
So we take this image, we rotate it,

11:21:34.680 --> 11:21:36.080
we have this other image,

11:21:36.080 --> 11:21:39.240
the same level obviously still parasitized.

11:21:39.240 --> 11:21:42.240
And then we flip again and get this image.

11:21:42.240 --> 11:21:44.880
We flip, get this image, flip, get this image.

11:21:44.880 --> 11:21:46.520
We see that we now have,

11:21:46.520 --> 11:21:50.420
instead of just this one, we have four others.

11:21:50.420 --> 11:21:54.560
This actually means we're multiplying this by five.

11:21:54.560 --> 11:21:57.600
So this is 100,000 since we have it now,

11:21:58.680 --> 11:22:01.640
one, two, three, four, five examples

11:22:01.640 --> 11:22:05.360
for this single example we had initially.

11:22:05.360 --> 11:22:07.460
It should also be noted that

11:22:07.460 --> 11:22:10.720
there are many other data augmentation strategies

11:22:10.720 --> 11:22:13.300
for this kind of image data.

11:22:13.300 --> 11:22:15.960
So apart from flipping as we've just done,

11:22:15.960 --> 11:22:18.160
we could crop just a portion,

11:22:18.160 --> 11:22:20.080
we could add some noise to this data,

11:22:20.080 --> 11:22:21.960
we could modify the contrast,

11:22:21.960 --> 11:22:23.720
we could modify the brightness

11:22:23.720 --> 11:22:27.240
and carry out so many other operations.

11:22:27.240 --> 11:22:30.320
And there is no particular data augmentation strategy

11:22:30.320 --> 11:22:33.520
which works for all problems.

11:22:33.520 --> 11:22:36.840
This means that when you have a particular problem,

11:22:36.840 --> 11:22:40.200
you will have to try different augmentation strategies

11:22:40.200 --> 11:22:42.080
and then be able to select

11:22:42.080 --> 11:22:44.740
the one which works for your data.

11:22:44.740 --> 11:22:48.480
Now, that said, we have dropout.

11:22:48.480 --> 11:22:51.120
To better understand this notion of dropout,

11:22:51.120 --> 11:22:55.400
we'll consider this simple neural network right here.

11:22:55.400 --> 11:22:57.400
Now, if you could recall,

11:22:57.400 --> 11:23:00.120
the reason why we have models which overfeed

11:23:00.120 --> 11:23:03.700
is because we are working with very complex models

11:23:03.700 --> 11:23:06.080
with many parameters.

11:23:06.080 --> 11:23:08.520
Now, in order to reduce the complexity

11:23:08.520 --> 11:23:10.720
of this neural network,

11:23:10.720 --> 11:23:13.920
what we could do is take off, for example,

11:23:13.920 --> 11:23:16.700
this interaction between this neuron

11:23:16.700 --> 11:23:19.680
and all those previous neurons right here.

11:23:19.680 --> 11:23:22.960
So this means that when training our model,

11:23:22.960 --> 11:23:24.960
we are only going to consider

11:23:24.960 --> 11:23:28.160
that we have in this hidden layer

11:23:28.160 --> 11:23:31.560
just this two neurons right here.

11:23:31.560 --> 11:23:34.600
So all those connections here become useless.

11:23:34.600 --> 11:23:37.920
Now, this has the effect of simplifying our network

11:23:37.920 --> 11:23:40.780
as what we have as output.

11:23:40.780 --> 11:23:44.960
Now, that's after carrying out the dropout operation,

11:23:44.960 --> 11:23:46.240
looks like this.

11:23:46.240 --> 11:23:50.640
So we have now a connection which look like this.

11:23:50.640 --> 11:23:54.960
Now, this particular case is an example of a dropout.

11:23:54.960 --> 11:23:59.720
We drop our ratio equal 0.3,

11:23:59.720 --> 11:24:02.480
or let's say 0.333.

11:24:02.480 --> 11:24:05.480
As we're dropping out exactly one third

11:24:05.480 --> 11:24:08.120
of all the connections,

11:24:08.120 --> 11:24:11.420
or rather one third of all our neurons right here.

11:24:12.380 --> 11:24:14.600
And if r equal two third,

11:24:14.600 --> 11:24:16.360
what we'll be left with will be this.

11:24:16.360 --> 11:24:18.000
So we'll take this off

11:24:18.000 --> 11:24:21.180
and we'll be left just with this neural network

11:24:21.180 --> 11:24:22.400
to train right here.

11:24:22.400 --> 11:24:26.120
We see that we could leave from this very complex model

11:24:26.120 --> 11:24:31.120
to a simplified model via this dropout operation.

11:24:33.080 --> 11:24:37.720
And this has an overall effect of mitigating overfeeding.

11:24:39.020 --> 11:24:42.900
The next step we could take is that of regularization.

11:24:42.900 --> 11:24:45.000
To better understand regularization,

11:24:45.000 --> 11:24:50.000
suppose we have this model with weights WJ.

11:24:50.100 --> 11:24:53.560
So we have say n weights,

11:24:55.260 --> 11:24:59.080
and this weights are free to take up any value.

11:24:59.080 --> 11:25:00.820
As we've seen previously,

11:25:00.820 --> 11:25:04.640
the fact that this weights can take up just any value

11:25:05.980 --> 11:25:08.220
may lead to overfeeding.

11:25:08.220 --> 11:25:11.400
As now, this weights can be adjusted

11:25:11.400 --> 11:25:16.320
to fit on the training data in a very perfect manner.

11:25:16.320 --> 11:25:18.520
So this means that we could have a model

11:25:18.520 --> 11:25:21.800
which picks out each and every point like this.

11:25:21.800 --> 11:25:26.680
So we have this kind of model and we have this.

11:25:26.680 --> 11:25:30.280
So we have this model which picks up every point like that.

11:25:30.280 --> 11:25:35.280
Whereas if we restrain this weights

11:25:36.240 --> 11:25:39.320
to stay in a given range,

11:25:39.320 --> 11:25:42.280
then we may end up with something like this.

11:25:42.280 --> 11:25:43.800
So we may end up with a model

11:25:43.800 --> 11:25:46.320
which looks simplified like this

11:25:46.320 --> 11:25:49.240
because it doesn't have as much freedom

11:25:49.240 --> 11:25:51.680
as this other model.

11:25:51.680 --> 11:25:53.580
Now the problem with this model is

11:25:53.580 --> 11:25:57.740
if now you're putting this new data,

11:25:57.740 --> 11:26:01.720
you'll find that this will try to pull out like this

11:26:01.720 --> 11:26:04.760
and then your prediction will be somewhere around here.

11:26:04.760 --> 11:26:09.300
And so if, for example, we're having horsepower

11:26:09.300 --> 11:26:11.340
in the x-axis and then year,

11:26:11.340 --> 11:26:13.400
want to predict the price of a car,

11:26:14.540 --> 11:26:15.700
let's do same year.

11:26:15.700 --> 11:26:19.380
We have the price and then the horsepower.

11:26:20.720 --> 11:26:22.860
Then it happens that we have this car

11:26:22.860 --> 11:26:26.680
with this very high horsepower.

11:26:26.680 --> 11:26:30.900
This model, because it is overfitted on this training data,

11:26:30.900 --> 11:26:33.160
will predict this very low price.

11:26:33.160 --> 11:26:36.280
Whereas this model which is generalized

11:26:36.280 --> 11:26:39.820
on this training data

11:26:39.820 --> 11:26:43.380
will tend to predict a more reasonable price.

11:26:44.460 --> 11:26:46.140
Now it should be noted here that

11:26:46.140 --> 11:26:48.340
because this model was able to go through each

11:26:48.340 --> 11:26:51.700
and every point, we would have a training loss

11:26:51.700 --> 11:26:53.500
of almost zero.

11:26:53.500 --> 11:26:56.740
Whereas now when we give it this new data,

11:26:56.740 --> 11:26:58.420
it doesn't perform well.

11:26:58.420 --> 11:27:01.880
Whereas with this, we wouldn't have a training loss of zero.

11:27:01.880 --> 11:27:05.140
But when given new data,

11:27:05.140 --> 11:27:08.500
at least we're going to have reasonable predictions.

11:27:08.500 --> 11:27:10.860
So coming back to regularization,

11:27:10.860 --> 11:27:14.380
our aim is to ensure that this weights,

11:27:14.380 --> 11:27:18.500
because this model can be represented as this function

11:27:18.500 --> 11:27:23.180
and this function is made of this weights.

11:27:23.180 --> 11:27:25.780
And so since when we're doing training,

11:27:25.780 --> 11:27:30.060
our aim is to ensure that we minimize the loss,

11:27:30.060 --> 11:27:33.340
then we could include this weights

11:27:33.340 --> 11:27:35.660
in the computation of the loss.

11:27:35.660 --> 11:27:38.580
This means that we have our loss, which is now equal.

11:27:38.580 --> 11:27:40.820
The loss would have normally

11:27:40.820 --> 11:27:44.300
plus the regularization constant

11:27:44.300 --> 11:27:48.460
times the sum of this weights of each and every weight square.

11:27:48.460 --> 11:27:51.380
Now this is known as L2 regularization.

11:27:51.380 --> 11:27:53.900
Whereas here we have L1 regularization

11:27:53.900 --> 11:27:56.540
where we're summing up the absolute value

11:27:56.540 --> 11:27:58.020
of each and every weight.

11:27:58.020 --> 11:28:00.180
For now, we're just going to explain

11:28:00.180 --> 11:28:03.620
how regularization helps in mitigating

11:28:03.620 --> 11:28:04.980
that problem of overfitting

11:28:04.980 --> 11:28:08.560
by restraining this weights in a given range.

11:28:08.560 --> 11:28:13.560
So let's have your loss equal L does initial loss

11:28:15.140 --> 11:28:17.380
plus let's call this R.

11:28:18.340 --> 11:28:22.280
Now, if our aim is to minimize this loss,

11:28:22.280 --> 11:28:26.360
then obviously this L will be minimized

11:28:26.360 --> 11:28:29.080
and R will be minimized.

11:28:29.080 --> 11:28:31.860
And so if we're trying to minimize this year

11:28:32.900 --> 11:28:35.180
or this sum in general,

11:28:35.180 --> 11:28:37.820
then it would have that overall effect

11:28:37.820 --> 11:28:42.820
of restraining these weights in a given range.

11:28:43.720 --> 11:28:47.700
Especially as we know that when we square very large values,

11:28:47.700 --> 11:28:50.300
these values become even larger.

11:28:50.300 --> 11:28:51.860
And so to avoid this,

11:28:51.860 --> 11:28:56.780
our weights will tend to take up smaller values

11:28:56.780 --> 11:28:59.760
which fall on the smaller range.

11:28:59.760 --> 11:29:04.600
This L2 regularization is also known as weight decay.

11:29:04.600 --> 11:29:06.700
It should be noted that the main difference

11:29:06.700 --> 11:29:10.100
between this L2 regularization and L1 regularization

11:29:10.100 --> 11:29:14.660
is that in trying to restrain the range of values

11:29:14.660 --> 11:29:16.560
which this weights can take up,

11:29:16.560 --> 11:29:20.480
the L1 regularization has that negative effect

11:29:20.480 --> 11:29:24.340
of making many of those weights

11:29:24.340 --> 11:29:27.100
to take up values around zero.

11:29:27.100 --> 11:29:29.620
That's values very, very small

11:29:29.620 --> 11:29:32.780
or take up many zero values.

11:29:32.780 --> 11:29:35.500
So this will lead to sparse models

11:29:35.500 --> 11:29:38.420
as compared to the L2 regularization.

11:29:38.420 --> 11:29:39.580
And that's why in practice

11:29:39.580 --> 11:29:42.660
we generally use the L2 regularization.

11:29:43.780 --> 11:29:46.180
That said, we move on to L stopping,

11:29:46.180 --> 11:29:47.900
which we've seen already.

11:29:47.900 --> 11:29:49.920
In L stopping, as we have seen,

11:29:49.920 --> 11:29:53.540
if we have our model, let's say we have precision,

11:29:54.460 --> 11:29:57.560
validation precision and then the training precision

11:29:57.560 --> 11:29:58.980
which keeps on increasing.

11:29:58.980 --> 11:30:03.420
And then we have this limit year of one or 100%.

11:30:03.420 --> 11:30:06.020
So we have the precision and we have our epochs.

11:30:06.020 --> 11:30:08.860
And then after a while this starts dropping

11:30:08.860 --> 11:30:12.340
and this starts dropping simply because our model

11:30:12.340 --> 11:30:16.940
is now trying to over feed on this data

11:30:16.940 --> 11:30:18.300
it's been trained on.

11:30:18.300 --> 11:30:22.020
And so we have to stop training once we notice

11:30:22.020 --> 11:30:27.020
that the validation performance isn't improving any longer.

11:30:27.780 --> 11:30:30.300
So this means that after a certain number of epochs

11:30:30.300 --> 11:30:32.020
we are going to stop the training.

11:30:32.020 --> 11:30:34.300
And we've seen this already in the previous section,

11:30:34.300 --> 11:30:36.740
we've seen it both to really go like this

11:30:36.740 --> 11:30:39.060
and then we had seen it practically.

11:30:39.960 --> 11:30:42.980
Then another thing to do is to reduce the size

11:30:42.980 --> 11:30:47.900
of the network or use a less complex network.

11:30:47.900 --> 11:30:51.940
Our next step will be to properly tune our hyper parameters.

11:30:51.940 --> 11:30:55.340
Hyper parameters like batch size, drop our rate,

11:30:55.340 --> 11:30:58.260
regularization rate and the learning rate

11:30:58.260 --> 11:31:03.180
can affect our model and dictate whether this model

11:31:03.180 --> 11:31:05.020
will overfeed or not.

11:31:05.020 --> 11:31:07.260
Now, if we look at the batch size,

11:31:07.260 --> 11:31:09.340
training with a larger batch size

11:31:09.340 --> 11:31:12.080
may speed up our training process,

11:31:12.080 --> 11:31:15.620
but working with smaller batch sizes

11:31:15.620 --> 11:31:20.420
have a regularization effect which help reduce overfeeding.

11:31:21.260 --> 11:31:23.460
And so according to Yann LeCun,

11:31:23.460 --> 11:31:28.460
frames don't let frames use many batches larger than 32.

11:31:28.540 --> 11:31:30.860
For the drop our rate, we'll see this already.

11:31:30.860 --> 11:31:32.900
Increasing the drop our rate means

11:31:33.880 --> 11:31:36.980
we are making the model simpler

11:31:36.980 --> 11:31:38.840
and the regularization rate,

11:31:38.840 --> 11:31:40.780
increasing the regularization rate means

11:31:40.780 --> 11:31:44.500
we're reducing the effect of overfeeding.

11:31:44.500 --> 11:31:48.900
And then finally, picking too small of a learning rate

11:31:48.900 --> 11:31:50.840
may lead to overfeeding.

11:31:50.840 --> 11:31:54.820
So in general, we have some hyper parameters to tune

11:31:54.820 --> 11:31:56.960
and they are not only limited to this

11:31:56.960 --> 11:32:00.200
as you may have many other hyper parameters

11:32:00.200 --> 11:32:02.340
depending on your problem.

11:32:02.340 --> 11:32:04.940
Now the fact that normalization

11:32:04.940 --> 11:32:08.580
introduces extra parameters, mu sigma,

11:32:08.580 --> 11:32:11.980
which bring in some noise in the model

11:32:11.980 --> 11:32:14.260
has that regularization effect

11:32:14.260 --> 11:32:16.740
which help reduce overfeeding.

11:32:16.740 --> 11:32:21.740
So if you're including batch norm in your model,

11:32:22.180 --> 11:32:26.300
then you could feel free to reduce the drop our rate.

11:32:27.220 --> 11:32:29.380
Since this normalization layer

11:32:29.380 --> 11:32:32.580
already brings in that regularization effect.

11:32:32.580 --> 11:32:35.280
From here, we look at ways of mitigating

11:32:35.280 --> 11:32:37.480
the problem of over underfeeding.

11:32:37.480 --> 11:32:42.480
So with underfeeding, we could use more complex models.

11:32:42.480 --> 11:32:46.040
We could also collect more data.

11:32:46.040 --> 11:32:49.520
We see that this solution falls in the tool

11:32:49.520 --> 11:32:52.160
that's both overfitting and underfitting.

11:32:52.160 --> 11:32:56.880
Here is always a good thing to collect more data

11:32:56.880 --> 11:33:01.440
or more clean and representative data.

11:33:01.440 --> 11:33:02.740
So from here we have,

11:33:02.740 --> 11:33:05.040
you could also improve the training time.

11:33:05.040 --> 11:33:08.240
Now note that you could have this model,

11:33:08.240 --> 11:33:09.920
like let's take this,

11:33:09.920 --> 11:33:12.800
and then we have here you've trained

11:33:12.800 --> 11:33:15.440
and then you have the validation.

11:33:15.440 --> 11:33:17.680
So here you have the valve and then you have the train

11:33:17.680 --> 11:33:22.680
and then you've been training for over say a thousand epochs.

11:33:23.240 --> 11:33:28.240
And then you feel like the model may not perform any better.

11:33:28.560 --> 11:33:32.600
Now, several scientists have reported that many times

11:33:32.600 --> 11:33:36.380
they've given up on a model and then come back later

11:33:36.380 --> 11:33:38.920
after forgetting to stop the training process

11:33:38.920 --> 11:33:42.480
and notice that this model kept on performing

11:33:42.480 --> 11:33:43.920
were much better.

11:33:43.920 --> 11:33:47.280
So sometimes you don't have to give up on your model.

11:33:47.280 --> 11:33:51.280
So you could train or you could increase this training time.

11:33:51.280 --> 11:33:53.820
Then again, we have hyperparameter tuning,

11:33:53.820 --> 11:33:57.760
which could help in making your model more performant.

11:33:57.760 --> 11:34:00.120
And we have normalization,

11:34:00.120 --> 11:34:02.600
which stabilizes the training process

11:34:02.600 --> 11:34:05.480
and leads to better performance in the model.

11:34:05.480 --> 11:34:08.020
We now see practically how the dropout

11:34:08.020 --> 11:34:10.340
could be implemented with tensorflow.

11:34:10.340 --> 11:34:13.320
So here we have this dropout layer,

11:34:13.320 --> 11:34:17.040
which takes as argument the rate, the dropout rate,

11:34:17.040 --> 11:34:20.040
noise shape, and the seeding.

11:34:20.040 --> 11:34:25.040
To better understand how and why we need to use seeding,

11:34:25.120 --> 11:34:30.120
is simply in the case where one reproducible experiment.

11:34:30.340 --> 11:34:33.720
So if wanna apply dropout in this layer

11:34:33.720 --> 11:34:36.080
with a dropout rate of 0.2,

11:34:36.080 --> 11:34:40.320
then we'll be taking off one neuron out of this five neurons

11:34:40.320 --> 11:34:43.360
to make our model simpler and avoid overfeeding.

11:34:43.360 --> 11:34:45.680
Now in doing so, we may take this one,

11:34:45.680 --> 11:34:49.920
or this, or this one, or this, or this other one.

11:34:49.920 --> 11:34:51.620
So it's a random choice.

11:34:51.620 --> 11:34:54.040
Now, if we wanna fix this choice

11:34:54.040 --> 11:34:56.600
so that this experiment can be reproducible,

11:34:56.600 --> 11:34:58.280
then we can set this seed

11:34:58.280 --> 11:35:00.800
so that each time we run the experiment,

11:35:00.800 --> 11:35:02.520
it's going to be exactly the same neuron,

11:35:02.520 --> 11:35:04.880
which is going to be taken off.

11:35:04.880 --> 11:35:07.480
Getting back to the code, the way we could use this

11:35:07.480 --> 11:35:11.360
is by importing the dropout layer.

11:35:11.360 --> 11:35:13.600
So here we just have this dropout,

11:35:13.600 --> 11:35:15.360
we run that and it's fine.

11:35:15.360 --> 11:35:18.440
And then we could include this dropout right here.

