and see what we need to pass in.

11:50:07.520 --> 11:50:08.120
So there we go.

11:50:08.120 --> 11:50:09.080
We have this image.

11:50:09.080 --> 11:50:12.120
We pass in the lower and the upper limit.

11:50:12.120 --> 11:50:16.680
So here, let's find this lower upper limit.

11:50:16.680 --> 11:50:19.160
We are told here, we're going to get an error

11:50:19.160 --> 11:50:22.400
if the upper is less than the lower, which is logical.

11:50:22.400 --> 11:50:24.520
The lower should be less than the upper.

11:50:24.520 --> 11:50:26.200
And then if the lower is less than 0,

11:50:26.200 --> 11:50:28.840
so we have to ensure that we're dealing with values greater

11:50:28.840 --> 11:50:30.200
or equal to 0.

11:50:30.200 --> 11:50:33.880
So getting back here, we have saturation, that.

11:50:33.880 --> 11:50:36.400
And then we have lower.

11:50:36.400 --> 11:50:37.480
Let's say 2.

11:50:37.480 --> 11:50:41.680
And then upper, let's say 12.

11:50:41.680 --> 11:50:42.800
Take this off.

11:50:42.800 --> 11:50:46.480
Run again and visualize.

11:50:46.480 --> 11:50:47.480
OK, so that's fine.

11:50:47.480 --> 11:50:50.520
So you see, we've added some random saturation.

11:50:50.520 --> 11:50:52.360
And this is what we get.

11:50:52.360 --> 11:50:54.080
We could obviously reduce this.

11:50:54.080 --> 11:50:58.200
We could make this fall in the range 0, 1.

11:50:58.200 --> 11:51:00.960
And here is what we get.

11:51:00.960 --> 11:51:04.200
OK, we now check out on cropping.

11:51:04.200 --> 11:51:07.080
Let's check out the central crop.

11:51:07.080 --> 11:51:12.040
Here we have central crop, central crop,

11:51:12.040 --> 11:51:15.200
and the original image.

11:51:15.200 --> 11:51:16.200
So that's what we have.

11:51:16.200 --> 11:51:19.880
Let's make sure we understand exactly how it's

11:51:19.880 --> 11:51:21.240
meant to be used.

11:51:21.240 --> 11:51:22.440
So here we have this.

11:51:22.440 --> 11:51:24.800
And then there's a central fraction.

11:51:24.800 --> 11:51:27.320
So here they explain how this works.

11:51:27.320 --> 11:51:33.560
Now, where x is the central 50% of the image,

11:51:33.560 --> 11:51:36.320
it is a float which lies between 0 and 1.

11:51:36.320 --> 11:51:39.600
As you can see here, 0.5 has been picked.

11:51:39.600 --> 11:51:42.560
Meaning that we're going to pick 50% of the image.

11:51:42.560 --> 11:51:45.400
But this portion of the image we're

11:51:45.400 --> 11:51:48.560
going to pick has to be centralized,

11:51:48.560 --> 11:51:50.920
has to be surrounded in the center.

11:51:50.920 --> 11:51:54.040
So that said, we've understood how that works.

11:51:54.040 --> 11:51:57.960
We can now put that, let's say, 0.3.

11:51:57.960 --> 11:52:01.160
Run that and see what we get.

11:52:01.160 --> 11:52:04.360
OK, so you see, we get this portion right here.

11:52:04.360 --> 11:52:08.320
Now, what if we expand this to, like, say, 0.8?

11:52:08.320 --> 11:52:08.840
We should.

11:52:08.840 --> 11:52:12.480
We expect to have some black regions now.

11:52:12.480 --> 11:52:16.400
Run that, and you see, we have some black regions.

11:52:16.400 --> 11:52:19.080
So it's actually centralized.

11:52:19.080 --> 11:52:20.680
Let's take this off.

11:52:20.680 --> 11:52:22.240
You see, it's actually centralized.

11:52:22.240 --> 11:52:25.920
And the reason why we have something like this,

11:52:25.920 --> 11:52:31.520
it's actually cutting out like this and something like this.

11:52:31.520 --> 11:52:36.720
So this is what we get in here, something like this.

11:52:36.720 --> 11:52:42.200
OK, so this is what we get when we do this central crop.

11:52:42.200 --> 11:52:44.800
Now, the way we're going to integrate this augmentation

11:52:44.800 --> 11:52:47.480
in our data pipeline is going to be

11:52:47.480 --> 11:52:50.600
similar to the way we did with the resize rescale.

11:52:50.600 --> 11:52:54.880
So just like we used this map method,

11:52:54.880 --> 11:52:59.120
we are going to reuse this same method for augmentation.

11:52:59.120 --> 11:53:03.760
Here, let's suppose we are going to add this code

11:53:03.760 --> 11:53:09.600
and then define our augment method.

11:53:09.600 --> 11:53:11.000
So we have this.

11:53:11.000 --> 11:53:12.480
Let's put this down.

11:53:12.480 --> 11:53:17.960
And OK, so let's just have your augment.

11:53:17.960 --> 11:53:22.120
We have this augment method, image, level.

11:53:22.120 --> 11:53:26.200
And then what we'll do is we'll take the image.

11:53:26.200 --> 11:53:27.800
And then for that image, we're going

11:53:27.800 --> 11:53:35.080
to have the rotate 90 degrees of that image.

11:53:35.080 --> 11:53:42.600
Then next, we are going to have adjust saturation.

11:53:42.600 --> 11:53:47.920
And then pass in the image and specify the saturation factor.

11:53:47.920 --> 11:53:52.280
So here we have our saturation factor equal, say, 0.3.

11:53:52.280 --> 11:53:55.120
Now you could always feel free to visualize this

11:53:55.120 --> 11:54:00.440
so you don't get to poorly augment your data.

11:54:00.440 --> 11:54:04.080
That said, here we could have this saturate.

11:54:04.080 --> 11:54:05.200
Always don't copy that.

11:54:05.200 --> 11:54:08.560
Anyway, this flip left, right, let's come back to this.

11:54:08.560 --> 11:54:12.080
We have this adjust saturation.

11:54:12.080 --> 11:54:14.360
We could check that out.

11:54:14.360 --> 11:54:17.320
And let's have it here.

11:54:17.320 --> 11:54:19.000
OK, we have that.

11:54:19.000 --> 11:54:22.520
So we run this and visualize to see exactly

11:54:22.520 --> 11:54:24.600
what it's going to look like.

11:54:24.600 --> 11:54:29.520
We modify that image to original image and then run it again.

11:54:29.520 --> 11:54:31.440
OK, so this is what we get.

11:54:31.440 --> 11:54:32.280
Now that's fine.

11:54:32.280 --> 11:54:35.600
We could also include the flip left, right.

11:54:35.600 --> 11:54:43.480
So here we have image, tf.image, flip left, right.

11:54:43.480 --> 11:54:49.600
And then what we do is we could return the image and the level.

11:54:49.600 --> 11:54:51.480
So that's fine.

11:54:51.480 --> 11:54:54.780
Then we could also just simply include this resize

11:54:54.780 --> 11:54:55.960
with scale in this augment.

11:54:55.960 --> 11:55:03.960
So here we have image and the level equal resize with scale,

11:55:03.960 --> 11:55:06.760
which takes in the image and the level.

11:55:06.760 --> 11:55:10.520
So we resize and rescale before doing the augmentation.

11:55:10.520 --> 11:55:11.560
So that's it.

11:55:11.560 --> 11:55:14.360
Now we have this augments meta defined.

11:55:14.360 --> 11:55:17.120
We could now, instead of doing this here,

11:55:17.120 --> 11:55:18.640
so here we have augments.

11:55:21.640 --> 11:55:24.400
Now another thing we want to do before doing the map

11:55:24.400 --> 11:55:26.080
is actually shuffling.

11:55:26.080 --> 11:55:29.000
So we'll modify the order in which we're doing things.

11:55:29.000 --> 11:55:34.160
Now here we have this train data set, could have that.

11:55:34.160 --> 11:55:39.160
And then we have the train data set right here.

11:55:39.160 --> 11:55:44.880
.shuffle,.match, and.refresh.

11:55:44.880 --> 11:55:46.120
So yeah, that's what we have.

11:55:46.120 --> 11:55:48.920
And then we just include the mapping right here.

11:55:48.920 --> 11:55:51.200
So instead of doing the mapping before the shuffling,

11:55:51.200 --> 11:55:54.400
we'll just do it after the shuffling.

11:55:54.400 --> 11:55:55.320
And there we go.

11:55:55.320 --> 11:55:59.920
Let's now have this, it's basically this with augments.

11:55:59.920 --> 11:56:01.480
So we just have that.

11:56:01.480 --> 11:56:03.280
And then there we go.

11:56:03.280 --> 11:56:04.880
We have this map.

11:56:04.880 --> 11:56:07.720
OK, so we now have that for the training.

11:56:07.720 --> 11:56:10.520
And then we could close this up.

11:56:10.520 --> 11:56:12.960
So that's fine.

11:56:12.960 --> 11:56:15.840
We repeat the same process for the validation and the testing.

11:56:15.840 --> 11:56:19.400
So yeah, we're going to modify just as we did with the training.

11:56:19.400 --> 11:56:23.680
We have here our validation.

11:56:23.680 --> 11:56:29.000
Then we include this map right here, map, resize, rescale.

11:56:29.000 --> 11:56:31.600
Now notice how we are not augmenting the validation

11:56:31.600 --> 11:56:33.000
and test data sets.

11:56:33.000 --> 11:56:34.840
So here we have the validation.

11:56:34.840 --> 11:56:39.520
And then we now do the same for the testing.

11:56:39.520 --> 11:56:43.760
Anyway, for the testing, we only did the map.

11:56:43.760 --> 11:56:46.520
That is, we only did the resize, rescale.

11:56:46.520 --> 11:56:48.120
So that's fine.

11:56:48.120 --> 11:56:51.880
We will now take this off, train validation.

11:56:51.880 --> 11:56:53.840
That's off.

11:56:53.840 --> 11:56:56.040
That's fine.

11:56:56.040 --> 11:56:58.680
OK, so here we have our test data.

11:56:58.680 --> 11:57:00.760
We have our train data.

11:57:00.760 --> 11:57:04.200
And then we have our validation data.

11:57:04.200 --> 11:57:06.000
At this point, everything is set up.

11:57:06.000 --> 11:57:11.200
We could now rerun ourselves so we start out with training.

11:57:11.200 --> 11:57:12.040
So that's fine.

11:57:12.040 --> 11:57:14.240
We will come back, get back to this.

11:57:14.240 --> 11:57:16.440
We have our data.

11:57:16.440 --> 11:57:22.640
We run all the cells and our model created.

11:57:22.640 --> 11:57:26.560
So yeah, we're going to work only with this sequential API

11:57:26.560 --> 11:57:30.840
and keep out all this other methods of creating models.

11:57:30.840 --> 11:57:36.520
Once we're done with that, we get right up to this level.

11:57:36.520 --> 11:57:40.480
OK, so now we get into training.

11:57:40.480 --> 11:57:41.760
That's fine.

11:57:41.760 --> 11:57:43.720
Compile the model.

11:57:43.720 --> 11:57:46.600
And we wouldn't use any callbacks for now.

11:57:46.600 --> 11:57:50.480
Take that off and start training.

11:57:50.480 --> 11:57:52.160
We're now done with training.

11:57:52.160 --> 11:57:55.320
And we'll see the results right here.

11:57:55.320 --> 11:57:59.520
So as you can see, the model performs very poorly.

11:57:59.520 --> 11:58:03.680
And we'll try to understand why the model doesn't perform

11:58:03.680 --> 11:58:09.320
as well as it performed without the data augmentation.

11:58:09.320 --> 11:58:13.160
Now, if you can recall, what we did for the data augmentation

11:58:13.160 --> 11:58:18.400
was that we rotated the image by 90 degrees anti-clockwise.

11:58:18.400 --> 11:58:22.240
We adjusted the image, the image's saturation.

11:58:22.240 --> 11:58:25.760
And then we flipped the image left right.

11:58:25.760 --> 11:58:28.440
But taking a look at the kind of data set

11:58:28.440 --> 11:58:31.120
we're dealing with, this saturation

11:58:31.120 --> 11:58:33.120
shouldn't be a great idea.

11:58:33.120 --> 11:58:36.400
And this is simply because if we look at each and every one

11:58:36.400 --> 11:58:41.360
of this, what differentiates a parasitized cell like this one

11:58:41.360 --> 11:58:47.040
and this uninfected cell is this patches we have right here.

11:58:47.040 --> 11:58:49.680
So you'll notice that with a parasitized cell,

11:58:49.680 --> 11:58:52.880
we generally have these kinds of patches.

11:58:52.880 --> 11:58:56.280
Whereas with an uninfected cell, we particularly have no patch.

11:58:56.280 --> 11:59:00.440
And with this, whereas here you could see these patches.

11:59:00.440 --> 11:59:03.760
And so rotating or flipping left right

11:59:03.760 --> 11:59:06.440
wouldn't change much about that.

11:59:06.440 --> 11:59:08.760
So that is acceptable.

11:59:08.760 --> 11:59:11.480
Now, the problem with modifying the contrast is

11:59:11.480 --> 11:59:13.480
when you modify the contrast, you

11:59:13.480 --> 11:59:19.240
tend to make the parasitized cells and uninfected cells

11:59:19.240 --> 11:59:21.000
less differentiable.

11:59:21.000 --> 11:59:24.280
And so it isn't a good idea in this case

11:59:24.280 --> 11:59:30.000
to modify the contrast or saturation of the image.

11:59:30.000 --> 11:59:32.600
Let's take this example so we could clearly

11:59:32.600 --> 11:59:37.520
understand why this saturation data augmentation strategy

11:59:37.520 --> 11:59:39.120
isn't a good idea.

11:59:39.120 --> 11:59:42.600
So yeah, right here, what we do is we just copy out this

11:59:42.600 --> 11:59:44.160
and then paste here.

11:59:44.160 --> 11:59:47.880
We take out just two elements from our dataset.

11:59:47.880 --> 11:59:57.880
And then here we have this 1, 4, and 2i plus 1.

11:59:57.880 --> 12:00:00.400
And we have the image show.

12:00:00.400 --> 12:00:03.800
From here, we create another subplot.

12:00:03.800 --> 12:00:04.840
There we go.

12:00:04.840 --> 12:00:06.280
We have that.

12:00:06.280 --> 12:00:09.160
And this one now is the saturation.

12:00:09.160 --> 12:00:13.760
So we have image.adjustsaturation.

12:00:13.760 --> 12:00:14.560
That's it.

12:00:14.560 --> 12:00:16.160
And then we'll pass in the image.

12:00:16.160 --> 12:00:17.360
And that's what we have.

12:00:17.360 --> 12:00:21.040
So here we have this first subplot and this other subplot.

12:00:21.040 --> 12:00:22.520
We have that, too.

12:00:22.520 --> 12:00:27.080
Now we could run this and see what we get.

12:00:27.080 --> 12:00:28.760
I'm using the positional argument.

12:00:28.760 --> 12:00:33.560
That is the 0.3, the saturation rate.

12:00:33.560 --> 12:00:35.400
So here we have 0.3.

12:00:35.400 --> 12:00:37.720
We're using exactly what we used previously.

12:00:37.720 --> 12:00:39.080
So here we have that.

12:00:39.080 --> 12:00:40.680
And this is what we have.

12:00:40.680 --> 12:00:44.160
In this example, we have both an uninfected cell.

12:00:44.160 --> 12:00:47.000
So we wouldn't actually see much difference.

12:00:47.000 --> 12:00:49.160
It's true this was modified.

12:00:49.160 --> 12:00:50.280
This was modified.

12:00:50.280 --> 12:00:51.880
But there isn't much difference.

12:00:51.880 --> 12:00:54.680
Let's take a parasitized cell.

12:00:54.680 --> 12:00:56.240
Let's rerun this again.

12:00:56.240 --> 12:00:57.920
Hopefully we get a parasitized cell.

12:00:57.920 --> 12:01:02.040
OK, as you could see here, let's take this off.

12:01:02.040 --> 12:01:05.080
As you could see, in this parasitized cell,

12:01:05.080 --> 12:01:07.120
what could clearly show the model

12:01:07.120 --> 12:01:10.400
that this is a parasitized cell is this patch we have right

12:01:10.400 --> 12:01:11.200
here.

12:01:11.200 --> 12:01:15.080
But when we adjust the saturation, it turns into this.

12:01:15.080 --> 12:01:17.840
And you'll notice that this parasitized cell now

12:01:17.840 --> 12:01:20.480
looks more like an uninfected cell.

12:01:20.480 --> 12:01:23.280
So the model isn't able to differentiate

12:01:23.280 --> 12:01:24.960
between these two clearly.

12:01:24.960 --> 12:01:30.320
So we see clearly that using the adjust saturation

12:01:30.320 --> 12:01:33.280
de-developmentation strategy isn't a great idea.

12:01:33.280 --> 12:01:34.920
Let's comment this part.

12:01:34.920 --> 12:01:36.400
And that's fine.

12:01:36.400 --> 12:01:39.120
Now we're going to modify our strategy.

12:01:39.120 --> 12:01:42.880
So just right here, what we had previously,

12:01:42.880 --> 12:01:44.320
we're going to take this off.

12:01:44.320 --> 12:01:45.840
So we comment this.

12:01:45.840 --> 12:01:49.200
And then we'll retrain our model.

12:01:49.200 --> 12:01:52.280
And so here we retrain our model after modifying

12:01:52.280 --> 12:01:54.800
our de-developmentation strategy.

12:01:54.800 --> 12:01:56.600
We run this again.

12:01:56.600 --> 12:02:00.080
And after training, as you could see, we get the results.

12:02:00.080 --> 12:02:01.840
Now we check out this loss.

12:02:01.840 --> 12:02:03.440
This is what we get for the loss.

12:02:03.440 --> 12:02:05.080
And if we look at the accuracy, we

12:02:05.080 --> 12:02:08.480
see that it looks more like what we had previously.

12:02:08.480 --> 12:02:13.040
Though we still have that maximum validation

12:02:13.040 --> 12:02:20.720
accuracy of about 94% and the trained accuracy of about 99%

12:02:20.720 --> 12:02:21.800
right here.

12:02:21.800 --> 12:02:24.280
And though the de-developmentation strategy

12:02:24.280 --> 12:02:27.880
hasn't closed this gap we had between the training

12:02:27.880 --> 12:02:31.600
and validation accuracy, we're seeing other sections

12:02:31.600 --> 12:02:34.920
how de-developmentation will be very instrumental

12:02:34.920 --> 12:02:38.400
in helping reduce this gap between the training

12:02:38.400 --> 12:02:40.600
and the validation scores.

12:02:40.600 --> 12:02:43.520
So far, we've been applying de-developmentation

12:02:43.520 --> 12:02:47.080
by using the image methods.

12:02:47.080 --> 12:02:51.560
Now the other way we could apply de-developmentation

12:02:51.560 --> 12:02:54.920
is by directly making use of Keras layers.

12:02:54.920 --> 12:02:59.760
So you come right here, tf.keraslayers, as you could see.

12:02:59.760 --> 12:03:03.200
And then you would notice that among those layers,

12:03:03.200 --> 12:03:07.960
we have those layers which permit us

12:03:07.960 --> 12:03:13.520
do modifications on images, like with this random contrast,

12:03:13.520 --> 12:03:18.120
which permits us randomly adjust contrast during training.

12:03:18.120 --> 12:03:20.360
Here we have the definition of this layer,

12:03:20.360 --> 12:03:22.440
this random contrast layer.

12:03:22.440 --> 12:03:28.240
And we have those attributes right here.

12:03:28.240 --> 12:03:30.080
Now, apart from this random contrast,

12:03:30.080 --> 12:03:33.080
we have the random crop, we have the random flip,

12:03:33.080 --> 12:03:35.520
and you could always get all these definitions,

12:03:35.520 --> 12:03:37.600
get back to, yeah, let's have this flip.

12:03:37.600 --> 12:03:40.120
Now the mode horizontal and vertical,

12:03:40.120 --> 12:03:43.280
so we're going to flip it both horizontally and vertically.

12:03:43.280 --> 12:03:46.360
We could also have other modes like horizontal,

12:03:46.360 --> 12:03:49.640
that's kind of a left-right flip.

12:03:49.640 --> 12:03:52.400
We have vertical, kind of up-down flip.

12:03:52.400 --> 12:03:53.880
We have horizontal and vertical,

12:03:53.880 --> 12:03:55.520
which is a mixture of the two.

12:03:55.520 --> 12:03:57.440
And the default is horizontal-vertical,

12:03:57.440 --> 12:04:00.640
as we've just seen right here.

12:04:00.640 --> 12:04:02.920
We also have this random rotation,

12:04:02.920 --> 12:04:06.760
which you could see, random translation, random zoom.

12:04:06.760 --> 12:04:11.120
So you could randomly zoom the data you're training on.

12:04:11.120 --> 12:04:13.000
And you also have this resizing.

12:04:13.000 --> 12:04:15.400
So we had looked at resizing previously,

12:04:15.400 --> 12:04:17.160
but not actually under the documentation.

12:04:17.160 --> 12:04:19.840
So we did the resize using TF image.

12:04:19.840 --> 12:04:21.640
Now you could also do this resizing,

12:04:21.640 --> 12:04:23.920
yeah, this resizing layer.

12:04:23.920 --> 12:04:25.920
Now, here you have the height and the width,

12:04:25.920 --> 12:04:29.720
as we had, similar to what we had in the image.

12:04:29.720 --> 12:04:32.560
We could scroll down and check on others.

12:04:34.040 --> 12:04:35.760
We don't really find anyone here.

12:04:35.760 --> 12:04:38.720
We could scroll back up, and that's it.

12:04:38.720 --> 12:04:42.240
Anyway, what's important to note is that you could create,

12:04:42.240 --> 12:04:44.240
you could implement data augmentation

12:04:44.240 --> 12:04:46.640
via two main ways, using the TF image

12:04:46.640 --> 12:04:48.840
and using the Keras layers.

12:04:48.840 --> 12:04:51.920
Now, we are going to look at how to re-implement

12:04:51.920 --> 12:04:54.640
