12:56:47.380 --> 12:56:49.780
The cut-mix data augmentation strategy,

12:56:49.780 --> 12:56:51.820
though based on the fact that

12:56:51.820 --> 12:56:54.260
we are combining two different samples,

12:56:54.260 --> 12:56:56.260
is different from that of the mix-up.

12:56:56.260 --> 12:56:58.580
Instead, with a cut-mix data augmentation,

12:56:58.580 --> 12:57:01.180
we are going to take a random patch

12:57:01.180 --> 12:57:05.020
from one of the samples and attach to the other sample

12:57:05.020 --> 12:57:08.180
while modifying the levels accordingly.

12:57:08.180 --> 12:57:10.780
We've looked at how to implement data augmentation

12:57:10.780 --> 12:57:14.180
with TensorFlow and also how to implement

12:57:14.180 --> 12:57:16.820
more advanced data augmentation strategies

12:57:16.820 --> 12:57:18.780
like the mix-up.

12:57:18.780 --> 12:57:20.900
In this section, we'll look at this

12:57:20.900 --> 12:57:24.340
cut-mix data augmentation strategy.

12:57:24.340 --> 12:57:27.140
Here, if we suppose that we have these two images,

12:57:27.140 --> 12:57:30.740
image one and image two,

12:57:30.740 --> 12:57:34.020
we are going to randomly crop a part of this image.

12:57:34.020 --> 12:57:37.140
So just like you can see in this output here,

12:57:37.140 --> 12:57:40.580
they randomly crop this section from this image

12:57:40.580 --> 12:57:44.900
and then attach that to this other image

12:57:44.900 --> 12:57:47.380
such that what you have in the output

12:57:47.380 --> 12:57:50.540
is this one with this patch.

12:57:50.540 --> 12:57:55.540
So this is how this cut-mix data augmentation is implemented.

