14:18:33.860 --> 14:18:36.420
Hello everyone, and welcome to this new session

14:18:36.420 --> 14:18:39.900
in which we'll see how to switch between the ego mode

14:18:39.900 --> 14:18:42.180
and the graph mode in TensorFlow.

14:18:42.180 --> 14:18:46.340
We'll first start by understanding what these two modes mean

14:18:46.340 --> 14:18:49.300
and also when to use either of them.

14:18:49.300 --> 14:18:52.700
Then finally, we'll see how to switch between these modes

14:18:52.700 --> 14:18:57.700
by simply adding this TF function decorator right here.

14:18:58.180 --> 14:18:59.700
So far in this course,

14:18:59.700 --> 14:19:04.440
we've been building methods like this one in the ego mode.

14:19:04.440 --> 14:19:08.700
That is, we've been following a Pythonistic approach

14:19:08.700 --> 14:19:11.460
in creating these methods.

14:19:11.460 --> 14:19:14.820
Apart from this ego mode or this ego way

14:19:14.820 --> 14:19:19.820
of manipulating data, we also have the graph mode.

14:19:19.820 --> 14:19:23.700
In the graph mode, we have data which is passed

14:19:23.700 --> 14:19:26.820
into these different nodes right here.

14:19:26.820 --> 14:19:29.740
Now, these nodes are operations.

14:19:29.740 --> 14:19:33.660
And so when we pass in our data, which could be a tensor,

14:19:33.660 --> 14:19:38.060
this data gets modified and then passed to the next node

14:19:38.060 --> 14:19:41.600
and right up to this output right here.

14:19:41.600 --> 14:19:44.580
This means that if we consider this line of code

14:19:44.580 --> 14:19:48.380
where we have X equal Y times Z,

14:19:48.380 --> 14:19:51.420
TensorFlow is capable of converting this

14:19:51.420 --> 14:19:54.060
into a graph with a single node

14:19:54.060 --> 14:19:58.360
where this node will represent this multiplication operation.

14:19:58.360 --> 14:20:01.660
So here we have this multiplication operation

14:20:01.660 --> 14:20:06.660
and then we have the two inputs, Y and Z.

14:20:08.700 --> 14:20:11.980
Then our output X.

14:20:11.980 --> 14:20:13.380
Let's draw it this way.

14:20:13.380 --> 14:20:17.260
We have Y, Z, the multiplication operation.

14:20:17.260 --> 14:20:18.580
Then we have X.

14:20:18.580 --> 14:20:21.940
Now this X, this data we get can be passed

14:20:21.940 --> 14:20:26.100
into two other operators or two other operations.

14:20:26.100 --> 14:20:28.300
So here we could have addition

14:20:28.300 --> 14:20:30.640
and here we have subtraction.

14:20:30.640 --> 14:20:34.320
Then we could combine this to finally have,

14:20:34.320 --> 14:20:38.660
say an addition operation and then get the output.

14:20:38.660 --> 14:20:41.500
Now let us come up with some code

14:20:41.500 --> 14:20:45.560
which will represent this rest of our graph.

14:20:45.560 --> 14:20:50.560
Here we've had X and then we could have, for example, R

14:20:50.740 --> 14:20:55.740
which is equal X plus a certain constant.

14:20:57.600 --> 14:21:01.760
So X plus a constant, let's call that constant key.

14:21:01.760 --> 14:21:04.820
We have X plus a constant key, let's just say constant.

14:21:05.780 --> 14:21:08.500
So this addition we have here is actually an addition

14:21:08.500 --> 14:21:11.300
to a fixed constant and that's it.

14:21:11.300 --> 14:21:14.640
So here we have R and then we could define S

14:21:14.640 --> 14:21:19.640
to be equal Y minus this same fixed constant.

14:21:20.780 --> 14:21:22.500
There we go, we have S.

14:21:22.500 --> 14:21:24.020
Now we have S.

14:21:24.020 --> 14:21:28.580
We could get an output by adding up S and R.

14:21:28.580 --> 14:21:31.040
So let's call this output T.

14:21:31.040 --> 14:21:36.040
We have T which is equal R plus S.

14:21:37.320 --> 14:21:40.300
Now this addition here is different from this one

14:21:40.300 --> 14:21:42.580
as this one takes in just one input.

14:21:42.580 --> 14:21:45.260
Since this input is gonna be added to the constant

14:21:45.260 --> 14:21:47.700
and the subtraction here takes in just one input

14:21:47.700 --> 14:21:49.860
as this input is gonna be subtracted

14:21:49.860 --> 14:21:53.380
or the constant will be subtracted from the input

14:21:53.380 --> 14:21:56.680
which in this case is meant to be X actually.

14:21:56.680 --> 14:22:00.500
Let's take this off, this is X

14:22:00.500 --> 14:22:02.740
because here you have, after this multiplication,

14:22:02.740 --> 14:22:05.260
you have X and this is X that gets in this

14:22:05.260 --> 14:22:07.360
and the same exact goes this way.

14:22:07.360 --> 14:22:10.960
So here it's meant to be X and that's it.

14:22:10.960 --> 14:22:12.580
So here we have now S.

14:22:12.580 --> 14:22:17.580
Once we get S, we have S here, this tensor S

14:22:18.380 --> 14:22:20.240
and then here we have our tensor R.

14:22:20.240 --> 14:22:23.420
Now R plus S gives us an output T.

14:22:23.420 --> 14:22:26.300
So we see that under the hood,

14:22:26.300 --> 14:22:28.640
what TensorFlow is capable of doing is

14:22:28.640 --> 14:22:31.140
taking this Python code you write

14:22:31.140 --> 14:22:33.980
and convert it into a graph like this one.

14:22:33.980 --> 14:22:37.380
One advantage of working with the graph mode

14:22:37.380 --> 14:22:40.980
is that this now becomes portable

14:22:40.980 --> 14:22:43.940
or this code now becomes portable

14:22:43.940 --> 14:22:48.940
since in a case where you don't have the Python interpreter,

14:22:49.180 --> 14:22:52.300
you have now this data structure

14:22:52.300 --> 14:22:55.220
which could be used in any environment.

14:22:55.220 --> 14:23:00.220
Hence making our code or this method more portable.

14:23:00.660 --> 14:23:03.460
Another advantage is that since we now dealing

14:23:03.460 --> 14:23:05.940
with this data structure,

14:23:05.940 --> 14:23:10.260
it can be broken up into several or separate blocks

14:23:10.260 --> 14:23:13.860
hence making it easy for parallelization

14:23:13.860 --> 14:23:18.020
leading to faster computations on devices like GPUs.

14:23:18.020 --> 14:23:22.500
The good news here is in order to convert this method,

14:23:22.500 --> 14:23:26.180
for example, into this graph,

14:23:26.180 --> 14:23:31.180
all you need to do is to add this TF function decorator.

14:23:32.380 --> 14:23:34.700
And when once this is done,

14:23:34.700 --> 14:23:38.900
when TensorFlow gets this block right here,

14:23:38.900 --> 14:23:41.460
it does what we call tracing.

14:23:41.460 --> 14:23:43.220
During the tracing process,

14:23:43.220 --> 14:23:45.700
this graph right here is generated.

14:23:45.700 --> 14:23:49.900
And once generated, each time you'll be calling this method,

14:23:49.900 --> 14:23:54.900
you now have your inputs passed into this data structure

14:23:55.180 --> 14:23:57.340
and your outputs generated

14:23:57.340 --> 14:24:01.900
without you going through each and every step in this method.

14:24:01.900 --> 14:24:04.660
Getting back to the code, all we need to do right here

14:24:04.660 --> 14:24:07.780
is to include our TF function decorator.

14:24:07.780 --> 14:24:11.460
And this automatically is turned to graph mode.

14:24:11.460 --> 14:24:12.940
That's fine.

14:24:12.940 --> 14:24:14.140
Run this.

14:24:14.140 --> 14:24:18.740
And then for this augment, we just add TF function

14:24:19.700 --> 14:24:20.820
and that's it.

14:24:20.820 --> 14:24:22.180
So there we go.

14:24:22.180 --> 14:24:25.620
Now right here in this Route 90 class,

14:24:25.620 --> 14:24:29.180
what we'll do is at this level of this call,

14:24:29.180 --> 14:24:33.420
that is when we compute in this Route 90,

14:24:33.420 --> 14:24:37.060
we will have this decorator put out here.

14:24:37.060 --> 14:24:37.900
So that's it.

